Guide.content._register("w15", {"id":"w15","order":15,"region":"senses","title":"Observability","short":"Observability","tagline":"Make a wrong answer explain itself without leaking the data behind it.","story":"The answer was wrong and nobody can say why. A classic service fails with an error, but an LLM system returns a fluent 200 that happens to be false, spread across model calls, tools and several agents. This world teaches you to record enough to explain a run, keep customer text out of the places it should not be, and meter cost and quality without drowning in data.","oneLiner":"Record the shape of every run by default, keep content behind an explicit policy, and alert on metrics because metrics are not sampled.","youWillLearn":["Explain why LLM observability differs from classic service monitoring, and which signals matter","Name the OpenTelemetry GenAI spans and attributes (chat, invoke_agent, execute_tool, invoke_workflow) and read their Development status honestly","Choose a content capture policy by sensitivity, debugging need, access control and effort, and know which flag controls which layer","Carry trace context across HTTP, MCP `params._meta` and A2A, and spot where hosted MCP tools break the chain","Sample traces without distorting totals, and count tokens with counters instead of sampled spans","Pick SLIs, SLOs and a backend (Foundry with Application Insights, Aspire, Langfuse, LangSmith, Phoenix) for your constraints","Defend a debuggable and private multi-agent design"],"minutes":100,"prereqs":["w09","w11"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w15-fc-001","front":"Why is a green trace not proof of a good answer?","back":"A wrong answer is a fluent success: HTTP 200 and no `error.type`. You need finish reasons, tool calls, token counts and quality scores on the run to see semantic failures.","tags":["basics"]},{"id":"w15-fc-002","front":"What is the status of the OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions on 2026-10-13?","back":"Development, not stable. They moved to `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai` on 2026-05-05. That repo has no tagged release, so pin by commit.","tags":["conventions"]},{"id":"w15-fc-003","front":"Name the span name patterns for a model call, an agent run and a workflow run.","back":"`{gen_ai.operation.name} {gen_ai.request.model}`, `invoke_agent {gen_ai.agent.name}` and `invoke_workflow {gen_ai.workflow.name}`. Agent Framework names its tool spans `execute_tool <function_name>`.","tags":["conventions","spans"]},{"id":"w15-fc-004","front":"Which attributes are required on a GenAI inference span?","back":"`gen_ai.operation.name` and `gen_ai.provider.name`. Common extras include `error.type`, `gen_ai.request.model`, `gen_ai.response.finish_reasons` and the `gen_ai.usage.*` token attributes.","tags":["conventions"]},{"id":"w15-fc-005","front":"What does the spec say about recording prompts and outputs by default?","back":"Instrumentations SHOULD NOT capture them by default. Content attributes are opt-in: `gen_ai.input.messages`, `gen_ai.output.messages`, `gen_ai.system_instructions`, `gen_ai.tool.definitions`, `gen_ai.tool.call.arguments` and `gen_ai.tool.call.result`.","tags":["privacy"]},{"id":"w15-fc-006","front":"What are the three content capture patterns in the spec?","back":"Record nothing (default). Record content on spans, suited to pre-production or telemetry that meets privacy rules. Store content externally and put references on spans, recommended for production.","tags":["privacy"]},{"id":"w15-fc-007","front":"Which layer does each content flag control?","back":"`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` (default false) is the Agent Framework flag. `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT` is the OTel and Azure SDK flag. `AZURE_TRACING_GEN_AI_CONTENT_RECORDING_ENABLED` is the older Foundry classic one.","tags":["privacy","agent-framework"]},{"id":"w15-fc-008","front":"What does `@trace_function` do regardless of the content flag?","back":"It always records parameters and return values, so it can leak data even when content capture is off.","tags":["privacy"]},{"id":"w15-fc-009","front":"How does trace context cross an MCP call?","back":"The client injects W3C `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage` into `params._meta` of the request, with unprefixed keys. The server extracts them as the remote parent. MCP revision 2026-07-28 reserves those keys.","tags":["propagation","mcp"]},{"id":"w15-fc-010","front":"Which MCP tools break trace propagation in Agent Framework Python?","back":"Hosted or provider-managed tools such as `get_mcp_tool(...)` on Foundry, OpenAI, Anthropic or Gemini clients and Foundry toolboxes, because the provider runtime makes the call. Client-opened transports do get `params._meta`.","tags":["propagation","agent-framework"]},{"id":"w15-fc-011","front":"What is the shape of a `traceparent` header?","back":"`00-<32 hex trace id>-<16 hex parent id>-<2 hex flags>`. W3C Trace Context Level 1 is a Recommendation (2021-11-23). Level 2 is a Candidate Recommendation Draft (2024-03-28).","tags":["propagation"]},{"id":"w15-fc-012","front":"Why alert on metrics and not on traces?","back":"Metrics are not sampled in Application Insights, so rates and counts stay exact. Traces are sampled, and the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro defaults to rate-limited sampling at five traces per second.","tags":["sampling","alerting"]},{"id":"w15-fc-013","front":"Head sampling versus tail sampling?","back":"Head decides at the start of a trace, cheaply, so it cannot keep every error trace. Tail decides after the whole trace is seen, so it can keep errors and slow traces, at the cost of compute and engineering effort.","tags":["sampling"]},{"id":"w15-fc-014","front":"Token usage counters versus per-operation histograms?","back":"Counters (`gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*`) are for totals and cost. The per-operation histograms are for percentiles and outliers and should not be used for total usage or cost.","tags":["metrics","cost"]},{"id":"w15-fc-015","front":"Are cached and reasoning tokens extra tokens?","back":"No. Cached tokens are a subset of input tokens and reasoning tokens are a subset of output tokens. Adding them on top double counts.","tags":["metrics","cost"]},{"id":"w15-fc-016","front":"Which limits bite when you attribute token cost at an API gateway?","back":"APIM `llm-emit-token-metric`: up to five custom dimensions, 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active time series per namespace, with the excess dropped silently. Azure Monitor allows 10 dimension keys per metric.","tags":["cost","cardinality"]},{"id":"w15-fc-017","front":"Why can Langfuse show nothing from a default Agent Framework Python exporter?","back":"The Python default is OTLP over gRPC, and Langfuse accepts OTLP over HTTP (JSON or protobuf) only. Set an HTTP protocol. This follows from the two pages, not from a vendor statement.","tags":["tools","agent-framework"]},{"id":"w15-fc-018","front":"Which SLIs suit an agent run?","back":"Run availability, p95 run duration excluding human pauses, p95 time to first chunk, tool error ratio, p99 model and tool calls per run, tokens per successful task and the online evaluation pass rate. They are design suggestions, not vendor numbers.","tags":["slo"]},{"id":"w15-t01-fc1","front":"Why is HTTP success not enough for an LLM feature?","back":"A 200 response can carry a fluent but wrong answer. You need run-level signals and sampled quality scoring as well.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t01"},{"id":"w15-t01-fc2","front":"Which signals describe the shape of a run?","back":"Spans for agent, model and tool steps, token usage, durations, finish reasons and tool-call counts.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t01"},{"id":"w15-t01-fc3","front":"What is the status of the OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Development. They moved to the semantic-conventions-genai repository on 2026-05-05, so pin versions and expect renames.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t01"},{"id":"w15-t02-fc1","front":"Where do the OTel GenAI conventions live since 2026-05-05?","back":"In the `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai` repository. It has no tagged release, so pin by commit.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t02"},{"id":"w15-t02-fc2","front":"Which two attributes are required on inference spans?","back":"`gen_ai.operation.name` and `gen_ai.provider.name`.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t02"},{"id":"w15-t02-fc3","front":"Why are metric names risky to hard-code?","back":"Older text defined a `gen_ai.client.token.usage` histogram. The new repository uses `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` counters, so builds differ.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t02"},{"id":"w15-t03-fc1","front":"What are the span names for a model call, an agent run and a tool call?","back":"`chat {model}`, `invoke_agent {agent}` and `execute_tool {tool}`. Workflows use `invoke_workflow {workflow}`.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t03"},{"id":"w15-t03-fc2","front":"Which span kinds do these operations use?","back":"Inference is CLIENT (INTERNAL for in-process models), `invoke_agent` is CLIENT for remote services and INTERNAL in-process, `execute_tool` and `invoke_workflow` are INTERNAL.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t03"},{"id":"w15-t03-fc3","front":"Why can .NET show no `gen_ai.*` spans?","back":"The agent or client was not wrapped with `UseOpenTelemetry`, or the provider did not `AddSource` the same name. The Harness wraps both layers by default.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t03"},{"id":"w15-t04-fc1","front":"Which metrics hold token totals, and which are for percentiles?","back":"Counters `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` hold totals. Per-operation histograms are for percentiles and outliers, not totals or cost.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t04"},{"id":"w15-t04-fc2","front":"Are cache-read and reasoning tokens extra on top of input and output?","back":"No. Cache reads are a subset of input tokens and reasoning tokens are a subset of output tokens.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t04"},{"id":"w15-t04-fc3","front":"What are the API Management token metric limits?","back":"Up to 5 custom dimensions, 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active series per namespace. Excess is dropped silently.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t04"},{"id":"w15-t05-fc1","front":"What does a `traceparent` header look like?","back":"`00-<32 hex trace id>-<16 hex parent id>-<2 hex flags>`. W3C Trace Context Level 1 is a Recommendation (2021-11-23).","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t05"},{"id":"w15-t05-fc2","front":"Where does MCP carry trace context?","back":"In `params._meta` as `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage`. MCP 2026-07-28 reserves these keys.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t05"},{"id":"w15-t05-fc3","front":"When does Agent Framework not inject MCP trace context?","back":"For provider-hosted or provider-managed MCP tools, because the provider runtime makes the call. Client-opened MCP sessions do get context in Python.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t05"},{"id":"w15-t05-fc4","front":"Does A2A define tracing fields?","back":"No. Its guidance says to propagate W3C Trace Context over HTTP and to log `taskId`, `sessionId` and correlation ids.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t05"},{"id":"w15-t06-fc1","front":"What is the default for content capture in the GenAI conventions?","back":"Off. Instrumentations SHOULD NOT capture content by default. The content attributes are opt-in.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t06"},{"id":"w15-t06-fc2","front":"Which content pattern is recommended for production?","back":"Store content externally and record references on spans. Content on spans suits pre-production or data you may freely keep.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t06"},{"id":"w15-t06-fc3","front":"What does `@trace_function` ignore?","back":"The sensitive-data flag. It always records parameters and return values.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t06"},{"id":"w15-t07-fc1","front":"What is the difference between head and tail sampling?","back":"Head decides at the start of a trace and cannot see errors. Tail decides after the trace completes, so it can keep errors and slow runs, but needs a stateful collector.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t07"},{"id":"w15-t07-fc2","front":"What does the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro sample by default?","back":"Rate-limited sampling at five traces per second for Azure Monitor export. Bursts above that are dropped.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t07"},{"id":"w15-t07-fc3","front":"Why alert on metrics instead of traces?","back":"Metrics are not sampled, so counts and rates are accurate. Use traces to explain an alert.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t07"},{"id":"w15-t08-fc1","front":"Which Foundry agent types have GA server-side tracing?","back":"Prompt and hosted agents. Workflow and external agents, and client-side tracing, are preview (checked 2026-10-13).","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t08"},{"id":"w15-t08-fc2","front":"How long does the Foundry portal Traces tab show data?","back":"90 days. Backend retention follows your Application Insights settings.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t08"},{"id":"w15-t08-fc3","front":"Where do GenAI spans land in Application Insights?","back":"In the `dependencies` table, with attributes in `customDimensions`.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t08"},{"id":"w15-t09-fc1","front":"Which spans does Agent Framework emit?","back":"`invoke_agent <agent_name>`, `chat <model_name>` and `execute_tool <function_name>`, plus workflow, executor and edge spans.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t09"},{"id":"w15-t09-fc2","front":"What changed in python-1.6.0 (2026-05-21) for telemetry?","back":"Instrumentation became on by default. Spans are created, but no exporter is installed and sensitive data stays off.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t09"},{"id":"w15-t09-fc3","front":"Why can a default Python agent send nothing to Langfuse?","back":"The default OTLP protocol is gRPC and Langfuse accepts only OTLP over HTTP. Set the protocol to http.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t09"},{"id":"w15-t10-fc1","front":"What is burn rate?","back":"Observed error rate divided by the error budget (1 minus the SLO). A burn rate of 1 spends exactly the budget over the window.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t10"},{"id":"w15-t10-fc2","front":"What starting alert numbers does the SRE Workbook suggest?","back":"Page at 2 percent budget in 1 hour (burn rate 14.4) and 5 percent in 6 hours (burn rate 6). Ticket at 10 percent in 3 days (burn rate 1).","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t10"},{"id":"w15-t10-fc3","front":"Why alert on metrics and not traces?","back":"Metrics are not sampled, so rates are accurate. Traces explain an alert after it fires.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t10"},{"id":"w15-t11-fc1","front":"What is the difference between offline and online evaluation?","back":"Offline runs on datasets with reference outputs. Online runs on production traces with no references, using reference-free judges and safety checks.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t11"},{"id":"w15-t11-fc2","front":"What is the default cap for Foundry continuous evaluation?","back":"100 runs per hour (`max_hourly_runs`). Runs are skipped when reached. The feature is preview as of 2026-10-13.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t11"},{"id":"w15-t11-fc3","front":"What does Foundry intelligent sampling do?","back":"Dedupes, filters broken traces, aggregates and selects diverse traces with MinHash farthest-first, on local compute with no extra model calls (preview).","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t11"},{"id":"w15-t12-fc1","front":"Which transport does Langfuse accept for OTLP?","back":"HTTP with JSON or protobuf at `/api/public/otel`. gRPC is not supported.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t12"},{"id":"w15-t12-fc2","front":"How should you set the LangSmith OTLP endpoint?","back":"`https://api.smith.langchain.com/otel` as a base URL with an `x-api-key` header. Do not add `/v1/traces` because the exporter appends it.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t12"},{"id":"w15-t12-fc3","front":"What is Arize Phoenix built on?","back":"OpenTelemetry, with OpenInference instrumentation. It offers tracing, evaluation, prompt tools and experiments.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t12"},{"id":"w15-t12-fc4","front":"How do you stay vendor neutral?","back":"Instrument with OpenTelemetry and OTLP, keep content policy outside the vendor choice and use a collector to fan out when migrating.","tags":[],"topic":"w15-t12"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions (overview)","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/README.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"New home since 2026-05-05. Every GenAI convention carries status Development, and the repo has no tagged release.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r2","title":"GenAI client inference spans and metrics","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/client-inference.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"Inference span name, required attributes, usage attributes and duration metrics. Status Development.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r3","title":"GenAI agent and framework spans","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/gen-ai-agent-spans.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"invoke_agent, create_agent, plan and invoke_workflow spans and their kinds. Status Development.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r4","title":"GenAI spans and content capture","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/gen-ai-spans.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"Opt-in content attributes, the three capture patterns and the upload hook. Status Development.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r5","title":"GenAI token metrics","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/gen-ai-token-metrics.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"Usage counters for cost, per-operation histograms for percentiles, and the rename from the v1.41 histogram. Status Development.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r6","title":"MCP semantic conventions","url":"https://github.com/open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai/blob/main/docs/gen-ai/mcp.md","type":"spec","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"MCP span names, metrics and propagation through params._meta. Status Development.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r7","title":"Model Context Protocol specification, basic (revision 2026-07-28)","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic","type":"spec","publisher":"Model Context Protocol","note":"Reserves traceparent, tracestate and baggage in _meta as an exception to the vendor-prefix rule.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r8","title":"W3C Trace Context","url":"https://www.w3.org/TR/trace-context/","type":"spec","publisher":"W3C","note":"traceparent and tracestate format. Level 1 is a Recommendation (2021-11-23).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"A2A enterprise-ready guidance","url":"https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/enterprise-ready/","type":"spec","publisher":"A2A Project","note":"Says clients and servers should participate in distributed tracing with W3C Trace Context. The spec defines no tracing fields of its own.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r10","title":"Agent Framework observability","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/observability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Enabling telemetry, environment variables, spans, metrics, MCP propagation and local viewers. Listed as preview in the 1.0 announcement of 2026-04-03.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r11","title":"Agent Framework workflow observability","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/workflows/observability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Workflow, executor, edge group and message spans, and the attributes that appear only with sensitive data on.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r12","title":"Set up tracing in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/trace-agent-setup","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Server-side tracing: GA for prompt and hosted agents, preview for workflow and external agents. Roles, 90-day portal view and content recording flags.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r13","title":"Trace agents client-side in Microsoft Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/trace-agent-client-side","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Client-side tracing switches and the trace_function decorator. Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r14","title":"Monitor AI agents with Application Insights","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/app/agents-view","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The Agents blade and prebuilt Grafana dashboards, built on the GenAI conventions. Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r15","title":"Sampling in Application Insights with OpenTelemetry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/app/opentelemetry-sampling","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Default rate of five traces per second, fixed-ratio option, validation query and the note that metrics are not sampled.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"API Management llm-emit-token-metric policy","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/api-management/llm-emit-token-metric-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Gateway token metrics, custom dimensions and cardinality limits. Cached and reasoning token categories are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"OpenTelemetry for LLM observability (Langfuse)","url":"https://langfuse.com/integrations/native/opentelemetry","type":"tool","publisher":"Langfuse","note":"OTLP endpoint at /api/public/otel over HTTP JSON or protobuf. gRPC is not supported.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r18","title":"Trace with OpenTelemetry (LangSmith)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/trace-with-opentelemetry","type":"tool","publisher":"LangChain","note":"Base OTLP endpoint and the x-api-key header. A base endpoint must not include /v1/traces.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r19","title":"What is Arize Phoenix?","url":"https://arize.com/docs/phoenix","type":"tool","publisher":"Arize AI","note":"Open-source tracing and evaluation built on OpenTelemetry and OpenInference instrumentation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"Sampling (OpenTelemetry concepts)","url":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/sampling/","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenTelemetry","note":"Head versus tail sampling, when to sample and when not to, and the costs of sampling.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Implementing SLOs","url":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/","type":"doc","publisher":"Google SRE","note":"The method for turning SLIs into SLOs from a baseline.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"Monitor your generative AI application (module)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/monitor-generative-ai-app/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Latency and token-usage monitoring basics.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"Evaluating AI Agents","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/evaluating-ai-agents/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Tracing and evaluating agent runs with a third-party stack.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"Evaluating agents and workflows (Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/evaluation","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"evaluate_agent, evaluate_workflow and local checks, the hook for online quality scoring. Listed as preview in the 1.0 announcement of 2026-04-03.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Alerting on SLOs (Google SRE Workbook)","url":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/","type":"doc","publisher":"Google SRE","note":"Burn-rate alerting: the 2 percent, 5 percent and 10 percent budget starting points and multi-window rules.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Observability in Generative AI (Microsoft Foundry)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/observability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The three lifecycle stages and what post-production monitoring includes.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Monitor agents with the Agent Monitoring Dashboard (Microsoft Foundry)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/how-to-monitor-agents-dashboard","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Continuous evaluation, the 100 runs per hour default, sampling modes and alerts (preview).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Evaluate deployed interactions with the Microsoft Foundry SDK","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/cloud-evaluation-deployed-interactions","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Trace evaluation and how intelligent sampling selects traces (preview).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Evaluation concepts (LangSmith)","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/evaluation-concepts","type":"doc","publisher":"LangChain","note":"Offline versus online evaluation, reference-free online evaluators and sampling controls.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Python 2026 Significant Changes Guide (Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/support/upgrade/python-2026-significant-changes","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Dated breaking changes, including instrumentation enabled by default in python-1.6.0 (2026-05-21).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"}],"glossary":[{"term":"OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions","def":"Shared names for spans, attributes and metrics of model, agent, tool and MCP calls. All carry status Development as of 2026-10-13 and live in `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai`."},{"term":"Span","def":"One timed operation inside a trace, with a name, kind, attributes and a parent. A run becomes a tree of spans."},{"term":"invoke_agent","def":"The span for one agent run, named `invoke_agent {gen_ai.agent.name}`. Its children are the model calls and tool calls of that run."},{"term":"execute_tool","def":"The operation name for running a tool. Agent Framework names the span `execute_tool <function_name>`."},{"term":"traceparent","def":"The W3C header `00-<trace id>-<parent id>-<flags>` that carries a trace across a hop. MCP carries it in `params._meta` and HTTP carries it as a header."},{"term":"Baggage","def":"Key-value context that travels with a trace across hops. MCP reserves `baggage` in `_meta` next to `traceparent` and `tracestate`."},{"term":"Content capture","def":"Recording prompts, outputs, tool arguments and tool results on spans. Off by default in the spec and in Agent Framework (`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA`)."},{"term":"Head sampling","def":"Deciding whether to keep a trace when it starts, for example a fixed ratio of 5 percent. Cheap, but it cannot keep every error trace."},{"term":"Tail sampling","def":"Deciding whether to keep a trace after all its spans are seen, so errors and slow traces can be kept. It costs compute and engineering effort."},{"term":"SLI","def":"A measured ratio or percentile of good behaviour, such as the share of agent runs without `error.type`."},{"term":"SLO","def":"A target for an SLI over a window, chosen from your own baseline. Burn alerts fire when the error budget is being spent too fast."},{"term":"OTLP","def":"The OpenTelemetry wire protocol, over gRPC or HTTP. Agent Framework Python defaults to gRPC, and Langfuse accepts only HTTP."},{"term":"Application Insights","def":"The Azure Monitor store for traces, metrics and logs. Foundry tracing writes to it, and its sampling and `itemCount` fields affect how you count."},{"term":"Usage counter","def":"A cumulative token metric such as `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens`. Use it for totals and cost, because it is not sampled the way traces are."},{"term":"Burn rate","def":"Observed error rate divided by the error budget (1 minus the SLO). A burn rate of 1 spends exactly the budget over the SLO window."},{"term":"Error budget","def":"The share of events allowed to be bad under an SLO, for example 0.1 percent for 99.9 percent. Alerts fire when it is spent too fast."},{"term":"Online evaluation","def":"Scoring a sample of live traces with reference-free judges and safety checks, because production runs have no reference answers."},{"term":"Intelligent sampling","def":"Foundry trace selection that dedupes, filters broken traces and picks diverse ones with MinHash farthest-first, with no extra model calls (preview)."},{"term":"`params._meta`","def":"The MCP request field that carries `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage` so a tool call joins the caller's trace."},{"term":"`invoke_workflow`","def":"The OTel GenAI span for a graph or orchestrator that coordinates several agents. It is INTERNAL and not used for a standalone agent."},{"term":"`itemCount`","def":"The Application Insights field that says how many original items a retained, sampled item stands for. Weight sums by it."},{"term":"Aspire Dashboard","def":"A standalone local OpenTelemetry viewer run in Docker. Agent Framework docs use OTLP port 4317 and UI port 18888. For development only."},{"term":"Reference-free evaluator","def":"An evaluator that needs no ground-truth answer, such as an LLM judge for groundedness or a safety classifier."},{"term":"Runaway run","def":"An agent run with an abnormal number of model or tool calls. Detect it with `gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls` and `.tool_calls`."}],"levels":[{"id":"w15-l1","order":1,"title":"Name the span: what one wrong answer should leave behind","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"An answer was wrong and nobody can say why. Decide what each model call, tool call and agent run must record so that one trace explains it, and how much to trust the standard that names those records. The dials are not given.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"A green request can still be a wrong answer","level":2,"id":"a-green-request-can-still-be-a-wrong-answer"},{"t":"p","md":"A classic service fails loudly: a 500, a timeout, an exception. An LLM system fails quietly. The request returns 200, the text is fluent and the content is wrong, so error rates stay flat while quality falls. Cost scales with tokens, latency depends on how many model and tool calls the run made, and the evidence you need (the prompt, the retrieved text, the tool result) is also the data you must protect. Observability for these systems therefore records the shape of every run (spans, tokens, finish reasons, tool calls) and treats content as a separate, governed decision (topic w15-t01)."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w15-d1","title":"One agent run as a span tree","alt":"An invoke_agent span has two chat spans and an execute_tool span as children. The execute_tool span has a child span created by the MCP server.","src":"flowchart TB\n  A[invoke_agent drafter] --> C1[chat model-a]\n  A --> T[execute_tool search_docs]\n  T --> S[MCP server: tools/call search_docs]\n  A --> C2[chat model-a again]","caption":"Illustrative shape. The server span joins this trace only if context crossed the hop, which is the subject of level w15-l3. Topic w15-t03."},{"t":"table","caption":"Span names and what to record (status Development, checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Operation","Span name and kind","Record at least"],"rows":[["Model call","`{gen_ai.operation.name} {gen_ai.request.model}`, kind CLIENT (INTERNAL allowed for in-process models) [[r2]]","Required `gen_ai.operation.name` and `gen_ai.provider.name`. Also `error.type`, `gen_ai.response.finish_reasons` and the `gen_ai.usage.*` token counts"],["Agent run","`invoke_agent {gen_ai.agent.name}`. CLIENT for remote agent services, INTERNAL for in-process frameworks [[r3]]","`gen_ai.agent.name`, `gen_ai.agent.id`, `gen_ai.conversation.id`"],["Workflow run","`invoke_workflow {gen_ai.workflow.name}`, INTERNAL, for graphs or orchestrators that coordinate several agents [[r3]]","Agent Framework's own spans are `workflow.build`, `workflow.run` (Python) or `workflow.session` (.NET, Go), `executor.process {executor_id}`, `edge_group.process`, `message.send` [[r11]]"],["Tool call","`execute_tool <function_name>` in Agent Framework [[r10]]","Tool name and duration. Arguments and results are content, so they are opt-in (level w15-l2)"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Agent Framework's built-in telemetry follows these names: `invoke_agent <agent_name>` at the top, `chat <model_name>` per model call and `execute_tool <function_name>` per tool. It also emits the metrics `gen_ai.client.operation.duration`, `gen_ai.client.token.usage` and `agent_framework.function.invocation.duration` [[r10]]. Topic w15-t09 covers switching it on."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"How firm is the standard? Checked 2026-10-13","md":"Every GenAI convention (inference, agents, events, metrics, MCP) has status **Development**. The docs moved from `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions` to `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai` (PR 3696, merged 2026-05-05). Release v1.41.1 (2026-05-11) of the old repo still holds the full text, v1.42.0 (2026-06-12) holds only a Moved stub, and the opentelemetry.io page shows Moved. The new repo has no tagged release, so pin by commit. Agent Framework docs still link the old URL [[r1]]."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of the first dashboard","items":[{"symptom":"The answer was wrong, yet every span is green and the error rate is flat.","cause":"Semantic failures raise no exception. The trace has no finish reasons, tool results or quality scores to expose them.","fix":"Record `gen_ai.response.finish_reasons`, tool call counts and an online quality score as attributes or linked evaluation results, and look at long or odd runs by token count."},{"symptom":"A token panel built on `gen_ai.client.token.usage` goes blank after instrumentation is upgraded.","cause":"The v1.41 docs defined that histogram. The new repo defines usage counters named `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*`, while Agent Framework docs still list the older name [[r5]].","fix":"Check which names your build and your backend actually emit, and pin the conventions version before building dashboards."},{"symptom":"A .NET service emits no `gen_ai.*` spans at all.","cause":"Spans appear only after the chat client or agent is wrapped with `UseOpenTelemetry`. The Harness wraps both layers by default, a plain agent does not [[r10]].","fix":"Wrap one layer (not both), call `AddSource` with the same source name on the tracer and meter providers, and confirm with a one-hour query."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-l1-q1","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":1,"q":"Match each operation to its span name pattern.","pairs":[{"l":"A model (inference) call","r":"`{gen_ai.operation.name} {gen_ai.request.model}`"},{"l":"An agent run","r":"`invoke_agent {gen_ai.agent.name}`"},{"l":"A workflow or orchestrator run","r":"`invoke_workflow {gen_ai.workflow.name}`"},{"l":"A tool call in Agent Framework","r":"`execute_tool <function_name>`"}],"explain":"The inference span carries the operation (for example chat) and the model. Agent and workflow spans carry the agent or workflow name. Agent Framework names tool spans after the function it runs.","ref":"r3"},{"id":"w15-l1-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"A token panel built on the histogram `gen_ai.client.token.usage` goes blank after the team upgrades its instrumentation to follow the current conventions. Spans still look fine. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Spans are sampled, so metrics derived from them are sampled and sometimes empty","The conventions have Development status: the v1.41 histogram was replaced in the new repo by usage counters `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*`","Content capture is off, so token metrics are not emitted","Token metrics exist only for agents that call at least one tool"],"answer":1,"explain":"Names moved between the v1.41 docs and the new repo, which is what Development status allows. Metrics are not derived from sampled spans, content capture does not gate token metrics, and token usage is recorded for plain model calls too.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w15-l1-q3","type":"single","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Which statement about the OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions is accurate on 2026-10-13?","options":["They are stable, so you can build dashboards on attribute names without pinning anything","They live at opentelemetry.io under `/docs/specs/semconv/gen-ai/` with versioned releases","They moved to `semantic-conventions-genai` on 2026-05-05, and since that repo has no tags the practical pin is a commit","The old repo's v1.42.0 still holds the full GenAI text"],"answer":2,"explain":"Status is Development. The page on opentelemetry.io shows Moved, the new repo has no tagged release, and v1.42.0 of the old repo holds only a stub, while v1.41.1 (2026-05-11) holds the last full text.","ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r5","r10","r11"],"takeaways":["An LLM failure is a fluent 200, so record the shape of the run (spans, tokens, finish reasons, tool calls) and treat content as a separate decision.","Know the names: model calls, `invoke_agent`, `execute_tool` and `invoke_workflow` (Agent Framework also has its own workflow spans).","Every GenAI convention is in Development and moved repositories on 2026-05-05, so pin by commit and check which metric names your build emits.","In .NET, `gen_ai.*` spans exist only after you wrap the client or the agent, and wrapping both duplicates content."]},{"id":"w15-l2","order":2,"title":"Decide what the trace may hold: the content capture board","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"Debuggers want the full prompt and the tool result. Privacy and retention rules want none of it in the trace. Decide what spans carry, where content lives, who can read it, and which switch controls which layer. The dials are not given.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three patterns, from the spec","level":2,"id":"three-patterns-from-the-spec"},{"t":"table","caption":"Content capture patterns in the GenAI conventions (status Development, checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Pattern","What spans carry","Fits","Catch"],"rows":[["Record nothing (the default)","Metadata: names, models, token counts, finish reasons, durations, errors","Production by default","You cannot read the prompt that failed"],["Content on spans","Opt-in attributes `gen_ai.system_instructions`, `gen_ai.input.messages`, `gen_ai.output.messages`, `gen_ai.tool.definitions`, `gen_ai.tool.call.arguments`, `gen_ai.tool.call.result`","Pre-production, or telemetry that meets your privacy rules","Content reaches every trace consumer and every retention tier"],["Store externally, references on spans","A reference to content held in your own store","Production. The spec recommends it","The spec still lists a common way to record external references as TODO, so the convention is yours"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"p","md":"The spec says instrumentations SHOULD NOT capture content by default [[r4]]. It also describes an in-process upload hook that runs regardless of the span sampling decision and independent of the opt-in flags, so a content store needs its own access control and retention (topic w15-t06)."},{"t":"h","text":"Dial board: content capture policy","level":2,"id":"dial-board-content-capture-policy"},{"t":"dials","id":"w15-l2-capture","title":"Choose what the trace may hold","prompt":"Flip the constraints. Redaction is on the board as a real option with a real miss rate, not as a cure.","dials":[{"id":"sens","label":"Data sensitivity","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low: public or synthetic data"},{"id":"reg","label":"Regulated or personal data"}]},{"id":"debug","label":"Debugging need","values":[{"id":"rare","label":"Rare: metrics usually answer"},{"id":"freq","label":"Frequent: must read the failing prompt"}]},{"id":"access","label":"Who may see what","values":[{"id":"same","label":"Trace readers may see user data"},{"id":"separate","label":"Operators must not see user data"}]},{"id":"effort","label":"Engineering capacity","values":[{"id":"small","label":"Small team, no platform to spare"},{"id":"platform","label":"A platform team can build and run a store"}]}],"options":[{"id":"meta","name":"Metadata only (default)","summary":"Names, tokens, finish reasons, durations and errors. No content."},{"id":"spans","name":"Content on spans","summary":"Prompts, outputs, tool arguments and results as span attributes."},{"id":"split","name":"Content in pre-production only","summary":"Full capture with synthetic data before release, metadata in production."},{"id":"ext","name":"External store, references on spans","summary":"Content sits in a governed store. Spans carry references."},{"id":"redact","name":"Redact, then record on spans","summary":"A processor masks entities before export, then content goes on spans."}],"scores":{"meta":{"sens":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Safe, but you give up debugging power you could have had."},"reg":{"s":2,"why":"Nothing sensitive is recorded, which matches the spec's default."}},"debug":{"rare":{"s":2,"why":"Tokens, finish reasons, durations and errors answer most questions."},"freq":{"s":-2,"why":"You cannot read the prompt or tool result that failed."}},"access":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Access is not the issue."},"separate":{"s":2,"why":"Operators see only metadata."}},"effort":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"Nothing to build."},"platform":{"s":0,"why":"Nothing to build, and nothing gained from the platform either."}}},"spans":{"sens":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"Synthetic or public data is safe to keep, and the spec names this pattern for pre-production and compliant telemetry."},"reg":{"s":-2,"why":"Content reaches every trace consumer and every retention tier, against the spec's default of not capturing it."}},"debug":{"rare":{"s":0,"why":"You pay storage for content you rarely read."},"freq":{"s":2,"why":"The failing prompt and tool result sit on the span."}},"access":{"same":{"s":1,"why":"Anyone who may see traces may see content."},"separate":{"s":-2,"why":"Operators who read traces read user data."}},"effort":{"small":{"s":1,"why":"One flag per layer, nothing to build."},"platform":{"s":0,"why":"A platform team would build something safer."}}},"split":{"sens":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Fine when pre-production data is synthetic."},"reg":{"s":2,"why":"Regulated data never reaches the verbose tier, because pre-production holds none."}},"debug":{"rare":{"s":1,"why":"Production metadata is enough most days."},"freq":{"s":-1,"why":"Production failures cannot be read, so you reproduce them in pre-production."}},"access":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."},"separate":{"s":2,"why":"Production traces stay clean of user data."}},"effort":{"small":{"s":2,"why":"Two settings, one per environment."},"platform":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}},"ext":{"sens":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but it is more machinery than public data needs."},"reg":{"s":2,"why":"Spans carry references. The store has its own access control and retention, which is what the spec recommends for production."}},"debug":{"rare":{"s":-1,"why":"You built and run a store you rarely open."},"freq":{"s":2,"why":"Follow the reference from the span to the stored prompt and result."}},"access":{"same":{"s":0,"why":"Extra friction with no extra separation needed."},"separate":{"s":2,"why":"Operators see spans, and only a cleared group opens the store."}},"effort":{"small":{"s":-2,"why":"The reference convention, the store and the access flow are yours to design, because the spec still lists external references as TODO."},"platform":{"s":1,"why":"A platform team can build and run it once for every team."}}},"redact":{"sens":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Redaction adds work for data that did not need it."},"reg":{"s":1,"why":"Masked entities lower the risk, but a missed pattern leaks into every consumer."}},"debug":{"rare":{"s":-1,"why":"Processing cost on every span for rare reads."},"freq":{"s":2,"why":"Prompts stay readable with sensitive spans masked."}},"access":{"same":{"s":2,"why":"Masked content is acceptable for an audience that may already see user data."},"separate":{"s":0,"why":"Operators still read whatever the detector missed."}},"effort":{"small":{"s":0,"why":"A redaction step is yours to build and tune."},"platform":{"s":0,"why":"A platform team can run it and still faces the miss rate."}}}},"takeaway":"Every option wins in some corner of the board. External stores are the production recommendation, yet a small team with frequent debugging may still do better reproducing failures in pre-production."},{"t":"table","caption":"Which switch controls which layer","head":["Switch","Layer and what it covers","Default","Gotcha"],"rows":[["`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA`, `enable_sensitive_data=True` (Python), `EnableSensitiveData` (.NET)","Agent Framework telemetry: prompts, responses, tool arguments and results, and workflow `executor.input`, `executor.output`, `message.content` [[r10]] [[r11]]","False. Python instrumentation has been on by default since python-1.6.0 (2026-05-21), but no exporter is installed and sensitive data stays off","In .NET, wrapping both the client and the agent duplicates content on both spans"],["`OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`","The OTel and Azure SDK layer used by Foundry client-side tracing [[r13]]","Off","A different layer from the Agent Framework flag, so check both"],["`AZURE_TRACING_GEN_AI_CONTENT_RECORDING_ENABLED`","The older Foundry classic content recording flag","Off","Old posts still use it. The current page uses the OTel variable [[r13]]"],["`@trace_function`","Foundry client-side helper that traces your own function","Always records parameters and return values","It ignores the content flag [[r13]]"]]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of content capture","items":[{"symptom":"Customer text shows up in the backend although `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` is false.","cause":"A `@trace_function` decorator records parameters and return values regardless, a hand-written span attribute holds content, or another layer's flag is on.","fix":"Audit custom spans, check the flag of every layer, and prove it with a canary: put a unique marker in a test prompt, then search the backend for it."},{"symptom":"Prompts appear twice on a .NET trace and ingestion cost jumps.","cause":"Both the chat client and the agent are wrapped with OpenTelemetry while sensitive data is on.","fix":"Instrument one layer. The Harness wraps both by default, so check its settings (`DisableOpenTelemetry`, `OpenTelemetrySourceName`)."},{"symptom":"On-call cannot explain a production wrong answer because there is only metadata.","cause":"The safe default is working as designed, and no governed route to content exists.","fix":"Add the external store with references on spans, a request-and-expiry access flow and its own retention. Do not switch content capture on for everyone."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","difficulty":2,"q":"Which statement about prompt and output content matches the GenAI conventions?","options":["Content is recorded by default and sampling decides whether it leaves the process","Content on spans is the pattern the spec recommends for production, because backends encrypt attributes and apply retention rules to them","Content capture is controlled only by the Agent Framework flag, whichever instrumentation emits the span","Instrumentations should not capture it by default. For production the spec recommends storing it externally with references on spans"],"answer":3,"explain":"The spec says SHOULD NOT capture by default and recommends external storage with references for production. Sampling is not a privacy control (the upload hook runs regardless of the sampling decision), and each layer has its own flag, so the Agent Framework flag does not cover an Azure SDK or OTel instrumentation.","ref":"r4"},{"id":"w15-l2-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"q":"You must show that a canary string in a user prompt never reaches the telemetry backend. Select every item you need to check.","options":["Agent Framework `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` is false","`OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT` is not true","Custom `@trace_function` decorators and hand-written span attributes","`OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` is set","The exporter protocol is gRPC","Only one of the client and the agent is wrapped in .NET"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Each layer has its own content switch, and `@trace_function` and your own attributes bypass them. The service name only labels telemetry, the transport protocol does not decide what is recorded, and wrapping both .NET layers duplicates content rather than creating it.","ref":"r10"},{"id":"w15-l2-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"q":"A team captures full content on spans in pre-production with synthetic data. The product moves to production with customer data. What should change?","options":["Default to metadata, keep content in a separate store with references on spans, with its own access control and retention","Keep content on spans and set head sampling to 5 percent, because a smaller sample of customer data is a smaller privacy risk","Turn capture off entirely and stop investigating production answers","Move content into span events so it is no longer stored with the trace"],"answer":0,"explain":"Customer data flips the sensitivity dial, and the spec recommends external storage with references for production. Sampling reduces volume, not exposure, so each retained span still carries customer text. Dropping all content gives up debugging for no reason, and events are still telemetry stored with the trace.","ref":"r4"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r4","r10","r11","r12","r13"],"takeaways":["Content is opt-in by the spec and by Agent Framework (`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` is false by default). The spec's production pattern is an external store with references on spans.","Every layer has its own switch, and `@trace_function` records parameters and returns regardless, so prove privacy with a canary and not by reading flags.","Pick the policy by sensitivity, debugging need, who may see what and engineering capacity. The right answer changes when a dial flips.","Wrap one layer in .NET. Wrapping both duplicates content and cost."]},{"id":"w15-l3","order":3,"title":"Follow the trace across hops, and keep the volume down","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"A request crosses a gateway, an agent, an MCP server and a peer agent. Some hops split the trace, and the bill for traces keeps growing. Decide how context travels, where it breaks, and what you sample. The dials are not given.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Carry context across every hop","level":2,"id":"carry-context-across-every-hop"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w15-d2","title":"One request, three kinds of hop","alt":"A client calls an agent service over HTTP with a traceparent header. The agent calls an MCP server with traceparent inside params._meta. The agent calls a peer agent over HTTP with a traceparent header.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant A as Agent service\n  participant M as MCP server\n  participant P as Peer agent\n  C->>A: HTTP with traceparent header\n  A->>M: tools/call with params._meta traceparent\n  M-->>A: result\n  A->>P: A2A over HTTP with traceparent header\n  P-->>A: task result","caption":"Every arrow is a place where the trace can split. Topic w15-t05."},{"t":"table","caption":"How context crosses each hop","head":["Hop","Carrier and rule","What to check"],"rows":[["Service to service over HTTP","W3C `traceparent` and `tracestate` headers. Trace Context Level 1 is a Recommendation (2021-11-23), Level 2 a Candidate Recommendation Draft (2024-03-28) [[r8]]","Gateways and proxies must forward the headers"],["Agent to MCP server","W3C `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage` in `params._meta`, with unprefixed keys. MCP revision 2026-07-28 reserves them as an exception to the vendor-prefix rule [[r6]] [[r7]]. The MCP client span is the parent of the server span whatever the transport","Agent Framework Python injects them for sessions it opens (`MCPStreamableHTTPTool`, `MCPStdioTool`, `MCPWebsocketTool`) and not for hosted tools such as `get_mcp_tool(...)` or Foundry toolboxes. The docs describe this only for Python [[r10]]"],["Agent to A2A peer","HTTP headers. The A2A guidance says clients and servers should participate in distributed tracing with W3C Trace Context and should log task, session and correlation ids. The spec defines no tracing fields [[r9]]","Agent Framework's A2A client and server forwarding is not documented, so test it"],["Async hops (queue, background response, durable resume)","No source here. General OpenTelemetry practice is to carry `traceparent` in message properties and start a linked span","Verify with a test. Resumed work often starts a new trace"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"An MCP tools/call request carrying trace context","code":"{\n  \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n  \"params\": {\n    \"name\": \"get-weather\",\n    \"arguments\": { \"city\": \"Pune\" },\n    \"_meta\": {\n      \"traceparent\": \"00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01\"\n    }\n  }\n}","caption":"Shape only. The identifier values are the W3C specification's example. Format: 00-<32 hex trace id>-<16 hex parent id>-<2 hex flags>."},{"t":"h","text":"Sampling: keep traces whole, keep the totals honest","level":2,"id":"sampling-keep-traces-whole-keep-the-totals-honest"},{"t":"table","caption":"Sampling options (topic w15-t07)","head":["Option","Decides","Wins when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Fixed-ratio head sampling, for example 0.05","At the root, from the trace id, so whole traces are kept [[r20]]. Python: `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER` and `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG`, not the older `sampling_ratio` and `traces_per_second` [[r15]]","Traffic is high and uniform. If unsure, the vendor tip is to start at 5 percent","It cannot guarantee that every error or slow trace is kept"],["Rate-limited head sampling","The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro default is five traces per second for Azure Monitor export [[r15]]","You want a hard cap on cost","Bursts above the rate are dropped, so load tests look incomplete"],["Tail sampling in a collector","After most or all spans of the trace have arrived [[r20]]","You must keep every error, every slow run or runs with a given attribute","Compute and engineering cost. The Agent Framework docs do not cover it"],["Metrics, which are not sampled","Every call is counted","Alerts, rates and cost totals","No per-run detail"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"formula","title":"Estimate totals from sampled spans","expr":"estimated total = sum( value x itemCount ) over the retained spans","vars":[{"k":"itemCount","v":"how many original items one retained item stands for, about 1 / sampling ratio"},{"k":"value","v":"the attribute you sum, for example gen_ai.usage.input_tokens"}],"example":"At a 10 percent ratio itemCount is about 10, so 300 retained spans of 1,000 input tokens each stand for 300 x 1,000 x 10 = 3,000,000 tokens. Application Insights stores `itemCount` on every retained item, and the Learn query `RetainedPercentage = 100/avg(itemCount)` shows how much is kept. Counters from metrics need no weighting."},{"t":"failure","title":"Where traces break and numbers lie","items":[{"symptom":"A trace stops at the agent's `execute_tool` span and the MCP server's spans start separate traces.","cause":"The tool is a hosted or provider-managed MCP tool, so the provider runtime makes the call and Agent Framework does not inject `params._meta`.","fix":"Use a client-opened MCP transport where you need end-to-end traces, or join the two sides by `gen_ai.conversation.id` and accept the gap."},{"symptom":"A load test shows far fewer traces than requests.","cause":"The rate-limited default of five traces per second drops bursts.","fix":"Use a fixed ratio, or raise the rate, for tests. Do not read the trace count as a request count."},{"symptom":"The traces you wanted, the errors, are missing.","cause":"Head sampling decided before the error happened.","fix":"Add tail sampling in a collector for errors and slow runs, and alert from metrics, which are not sampled."},{"symptom":"Token totals computed from spans are far too low.","cause":"Retained spans were summed without weighting by `itemCount`.","fix":"Weight by `itemCount`, or use the usage counters (level w15-l4)."},{"symptom":"The peer agent's work appears as a separate trace.","cause":"Nothing forwarded `traceparent` over the A2A hop. The A2A spec has no tracing fields and the Agent Framework docs do not say whether its A2A client forwards the header.","fix":"Test with a known `traceparent` and look for its trace id in the peer's spans. Log task and correlation ids as the A2A guidance advises [[r9]]."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-l3-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"q":"Put the steps of trace propagation across an MCP tool call in order.","items":["The agent has an active span when it issues `tools/call`","The client injects `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage` into `params._meta`","The server extracts them and uses them as the remote parent","The server's span joins the same trace beneath the client span"],"explain":"An active span must exist to have a context to inject. The client writes it into `params._meta`, the server reads it as the remote parent, and its own span then becomes part of the trace.","ref":"r6"},{"id":"w15-l3-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"Traces are head-sampled at a fixed ratio of 5 percent, so `itemCount` is about 20. In one hour, 500 retained chat spans carry an average of 2,000 `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` each. Estimate the real input tokens for the hour.","answer":20000000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Weight each retained span by itemCount.","explain":"Each retained span stands for about 20 original spans, so the estimate is 500 x 2,000 x 20 = 20,000,000 tokens. Summing the retained spans alone gives 1,000,000, which is 20 times too low. A usage counter would have given the exact figure without weighting.","ref":"r15"},{"id":"w15-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"q":"An agent calls a Foundry-hosted MCP tool through `get_mcp_tool(...)`. Traces end at the agent's `execute_tool` span and the MCP server's spans start separate traces. Why?","options":["Head sampling dropped the MCP server's spans, because each service applies its own sampling ratio and the server's ratio was lower","MCP forbids `traceparent` in `_meta` because of its vendor-prefix rule for unregistered keys","A hosted tool is called by the provider runtime, so no `params._meta` is injected. Use a client-opened MCP transport","`baggage` must be set first, because the server accepts `traceparent` only when baggage is present"],"answer":2,"explain":"Agent Framework injects trace context only for MCP sessions the agent process opens. A hosted tool is invoked by the provider's runtime. MCP reserves `traceparent` in `_meta` as an exception to the vendor-prefix rule, baggage is optional, and head sampling would cut whole traces, not split them.","ref":"r10"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r6","r7","r8","r9","r10","r15","r20"],"takeaways":["A trace crosses a hop only if something carries the context: HTTP headers between services, `params._meta` for MCP, headers for A2A.","Hosted or provider-managed MCP tools are called by the provider, so the chain breaks there. Use a client-opened transport for end-to-end traces.","Head sampling is cheap and cannot keep every error. Tail sampling can, at a cost. Metrics are never sampled, so alert from them.","Weight sums over sampled spans by `itemCount`, or use counters, and treat the A2A forwarding as untested until you test it."]},{"id":"w15-l4","order":4,"title":"Meter the tokens, set the targets, pick the backend","kind":"problem","minutes":22,"brief":"Finance wants cost per team, the on-call engineer wants a target that tells her when to wake up, and the product owner wants to know whether answers got worse. Choose the meters, the SLIs and the place the telemetry lives. The dials are not given.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Count tokens where sampling cannot hurt","level":2,"id":"count-tokens-where-sampling-cannot-hurt"},{"t":"table","caption":"GenAI metrics by job (status Development, checked 2026-10-13). Topic w15-t04.","head":["Signal","Use it for","Careful"],"rows":[["Usage counters `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens` and `.output_tokens`","Totals and cost, split by `gen_ai.token.modality` [[r5]]","`input_tokens` includes cached tokens and `output_tokens` includes reasoning tokens. The cache read, cache write and reasoning counters are subsets"],["Per-operation histograms `gen_ai.client.inference.operation.input_tokens` and `.output_tokens`","Percentiles and outliers","They should not be used for total usage or cost calculations"],["Latency histograms `gen_ai.client.inference.duration`, `.time_to_first_chunk` (streaming only), `.time_per_output_chunk`","Slow models and the feel of streaming","Recommended buckets run from 0.01 s and double up to 81.92 s"],["Agent metrics `gen_ai.invoke_agent.duration`, `.inference_calls`, `.tool_calls`, and `gen_ai.execute_tool.duration`","Runaway loops and slow tools","Agent Framework docs list `gen_ai.client.token.usage`, so check which names your build emits [[r10]]"]]},{"t":"formula","title":"Full-price input tokens","expr":"uncached input = input_tokens - cache_read.input_tokens","vars":[{"k":"input_tokens","v":"gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens, which already includes cached tokens"},{"k":"cache_read.input_tokens","v":"the subset served from the prompt cache"}],"example":"A call reports 8,000 input tokens of which 6,000 were cache reads, so 2,000 tokens are uncached. Adding the cache reads on top would report 14,000. Cache writes are also a subset of input and may be priced differently, so split them out the same way."},{"t":"h","text":"Dial board: where does the telemetry live?","level":2,"id":"dial-board-where-does-the-telemetry-live"},{"t":"dials","id":"w15-l4-backend","title":"Choose a telemetry backend","prompt":"Flip the constraints. This board scores tracing and viewing. Compare evaluation features in chapters w12 and w14. Topics w15-t08 and w15-t12.","dials":[{"id":"eco","label":"Ecosystem","values":[{"id":"azure","label":"Azure-first, enterprise controls"},{"id":"neutral","label":"Multi-cloud or self-hosted, OpenTelemetry-first"},{"id":"lc","label":"Already on LangChain or LangGraph"}]},{"id":"stage","label":"Stage","values":[{"id":"dev","label":"Local development"},{"id":"prod","label":"Production"}]},{"id":"feat","label":"What you need in the tool","values":[{"id":"plain","label":"Generic traces and metrics"},{"id":"llm","label":"An LLM-focused UI with scoring and prompt iteration"}]}],"options":[{"id":"foundry","name":"Foundry portal with Application Insights","summary":"Server-side tracing after you connect App Insights to the Foundry project."},{"id":"grafana","name":"Application Insights with Grafana","summary":"Agents view (preview) and prebuilt Grafana dashboards."},{"id":"aspire","name":"Aspire Dashboard","summary":"A standalone local OTLP viewer in Docker."},{"id":"langfuse","name":"Langfuse","summary":"LLM observability that takes OTLP over HTTP."},{"id":"langsmith","name":"LangSmith","summary":"LLM traces and a runs dashboard, with OTLP ingestion."},{"id":"phoenix","name":"Arize Phoenix","summary":"Open-source tracing and evaluation built on OpenTelemetry."}],"scores":{"foundry":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":2,"why":"Server-side tracing turns on after you connect an Application Insights resource to the project, with no code change."},"neutral":{"s":-1,"why":"The no-code path covers agents hosted in Foundry. Elsewhere you export client-side, which is preview."},"lc":{"s":-1,"why":"Code that Foundry does not host needs the client-side route, which is preview."}},"stage":{"dev":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs an Azure project, and traces take a few minutes to appear."},"prod":{"s":2,"why":"GA for prompt and hosted agents. The portal Traces tab shows 90 days and the data sits in Application Insights [[r12]]."}},"feat":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"Traces and metrics are what it provides first."},"llm":{"s":0,"why":"Tracing is the verified part here. Check evaluation features separately."}}},"grafana":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":2,"why":"Prebuilt Grafana dashboards exist for Agent Framework, workflows, Foundry and coding agents, next to the Agents view (preview) [[r14]]."},"neutral":{"s":0,"why":"OTLP in from any language, but Azure Monitor is still the store."},"lc":{"s":0,"why":"Works through OTLP like any other app."}},"stage":{"dev":{"s":0,"why":"Needs an Azure resource, though a developer machine can export to it."},"prod":{"s":1,"why":"You control sampling and alerts, and metrics are not sampled. The Agents view is preview."}},"feat":{"plain":{"s":2,"why":"Traces, metrics and dashboards are the product."},"llm":{"s":-1,"why":"The Agents view covers runs, tool calls, models and errors, and sorts traces by tokens used. Prompt iteration and scoring live elsewhere."}}},"aspire":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":0,"why":"A local viewer, not a platform."},"neutral":{"s":1,"why":"Standard OTLP, any language."},"lc":{"s":0,"why":"Standard OTLP."}},"stage":{"dev":{"s":2,"why":"One Docker container: OTLP on port 4317 and the UI on port 18888, per the Agent Framework doc [[r10]]."},"prod":{"s":-2,"why":"It is framed as a local development viewer."}},"feat":{"plain":{"s":1,"why":"Traces, logs and metrics in one place."},"llm":{"s":-1,"why":"No scoring or prompt workspace."}}},"langfuse":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":-1,"why":"Not an Azure service, so telemetry leaves your tenant unless you run it yourself."},"neutral":{"s":2,"why":"OTLP in over HTTP, with cloud regions or a local deployment."},"lc":{"s":1,"why":"Receives OTLP from other libraries and has its own SDKs."}},"stage":{"dev":{"s":0,"why":"Needs a cloud account or a local deployment."},"prod":{"s":1,"why":"Works in production if the exporter speaks OTLP over HTTP. gRPC is not supported [[r17]]."}},"feat":{"plain":{"s":0,"why":"Built for LLM traces, not generic services."},"llm":{"s":2,"why":"Built around LLM traces. By default it keeps spans that look LLM-relevant."}}},"langsmith":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":-1,"why":"SaaS or self-hosted, outside Azure Monitor."},"neutral":{"s":0,"why":"Takes OTLP with an x-api-key header, so any OpenTelemetry app can send to it [[r18]]."},"lc":{"s":2,"why":"Built-in integration for LangChain and LangGraph apps, plus plain OTLP for the rest."}},"stage":{"dev":{"s":0,"why":"Needs an account or a self-hosted install."},"prod":{"s":1,"why":"Regional SaaS endpoints and self-hosted installs exist."}},"feat":{"plain":{"s":0,"why":"Aimed at LLM runs more than generic services."},"llm":{"s":2,"why":"Traces and a runs dashboard for LLM apps."}}},"phoenix":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":-1,"why":"Not an Azure service."},"neutral":{"s":2,"why":"Open source, built on OpenTelemetry and OpenInference instrumentation [[r19]]."},"lc":{"s":1,"why":"Auto-instrumentation for LangChain, LlamaIndex, DSPy and other frameworks."}},"stage":{"dev":{"s":1,"why":"Open source, so you can run it beside your code."},"prod":{"s":1,"why":"Arize also offers AX, a managed platform on the same open standards."}},"feat":{"plain":{"s":0,"why":"Aimed at AI applications."},"llm":{"s":2,"why":"Traces, evaluation and prompt iteration in one tool."}}}},"takeaway":"Every option wins somewhere. Because they all speak OpenTelemetry, the cheapest hedge is to instrument once with standard OTLP and keep the backend swappable."},{"t":"table","caption":"SLIs for an agent run. These are design suggestions, not vendor numbers. Topic w15-t10.","head":["SLI","How to measure","Signal"],"rows":[["Availability of runs","Share of `invoke_agent` spans without `error.type`","`gen_ai.invoke_agent.duration` with `error.type`"],["End-to-end latency","p95 run duration, excluding time paused for human approval","`gen_ai.invoke_agent.duration`"],["Time to first output (streaming)","p95 first-chunk time","`gen_ai.client.inference.time_to_first_chunk`"],["Tool reliability","Error ratio and p95 duration per tool","`gen_ai.execute_tool.duration`"],["Runaway detection","p99 of model calls and tool calls per run","`gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls`, `.tool_calls`"],["Cost","Tokens per successful task and the cached share","`gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` counters"],["Quality","Online evaluation pass rate on sampled traffic","Evaluation results (chapter w14)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Targets, alerts and online quality","md":"Choose each SLO from your own baseline with the SRE Workbook method, not from a copied number [[r21]]. Alert on metrics, which are not sampled, and use traces to explain an alert. For the quality SLI, score a sampled share of live traffic with the framework's evaluation hooks (`evaluate_agent`, `evaluate_workflow`) or a cloud evaluator, and watch the pass rate for drift (topic w15-t11) [[r24]]. A course on tracing and evaluating agent runs with a third-party stack is listed in the references."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of metering and export","items":[{"symptom":"The cost dashboard shows more tokens than the provider bill.","cause":"Cached tokens were added on top of input tokens, but they are a subset of them.","fix":"Use `input_tokens - cache_read.input_tokens` for the uncached share."},{"symptom":"A per-user cost metric loses series without any error.","cause":"The APIM `llm-emit-token-metric` policy allows up to five custom dimensions, 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active time series per namespace, and drops the excess silently. Azure Monitor allows 10 dimension keys per metric [[r16]].","fix":"Use low-cardinality dimensions (team, product, model) and read per-user detail from traces or logs."},{"symptom":"Langfuse shows nothing from a default Agent Framework Python exporter.","cause":"The Python default is OTLP over gRPC and Langfuse accepts only OTLP over HTTP at `/api/public/otel`. This follows from the two pages, not from a vendor statement.","fix":"Set an HTTP protocol for the exporter and point it at the Langfuse endpoint."},{"symptom":"LangSmith returns 404 for every export.","cause":"The base endpoint included `/v1/traces`, which OTLP/HTTP exporters append themselves.","fix":"Set `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` to `https://api.smith.langchain.com/otel` and pass the `x-api-key` header."},{"symptom":"Gateway token counts are low for streamed calls.","cause":"Interrupted streams give wrong counts, and some OpenAI streams need `include_usage` set to true.","fix":"Set `include_usage` and compare gateway totals with provider-reported usage."}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-l4-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"q":"A call reports `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens` of 12,000, of which `cache_read.input_tokens` is 9,000. There were no cache writes. How many input tokens were uncached?","answer":3000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Cached tokens are a subset of input tokens.","explain":"The input count already includes the cache reads, so uncached input is 12,000 - 9,000 = 3,000. Adding them would give 21,000, which double counts. The same subset rule holds for reasoning tokens inside output tokens.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w15-l4-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each need to the signal that answers it best.","pairs":[{"l":"Total spend by team this month","r":"Usage counters such as `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens`"},{"l":"Find requests with unusually large prompts","r":"Per-operation token histograms"},{"l":"Streaming feels slow to users","r":"`gen_ai.client.inference.time_to_first_chunk`"},{"l":"Detect a runaway tool loop","r":"`gen_ai.invoke_agent.tool_calls` and `.inference_calls` per run"}],"explain":"Counters are the sums to use for cost. The per-operation histograms exist for percentiles and outliers and should not be used for totals. Time to first chunk is the streaming responsiveness metric, and per-run call counts expose loops.","ref":"r5"},{"id":"w15-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"q":"Finance asks for token cost per user per day. The gateway can emit token metrics with custom dimensions. What is the sound design?","options":["Attribute metrics by team or product with few values, and look up per-user detail in traces or logs","Add the user id as a custom dimension on the APIM token metric, because the policy allows five custom dimensions per metric","Raise the Azure Monitor limits so that every user id gets its own time series","Compute per-user cost from sampled spans and report the retained total as it is"],"answer":0,"explain":"A user id has high cardinality: APIM caps a dimension at 100 unique values and 1,000 active time series per namespace and drops the excess silently. Raising Azure Monitor limits does not lift the APIM caps. Sampled spans must be weighted by `itemCount`, or the total is far too low.","ref":"r16"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r5","r10","r12","r14","r16","r17","r18","r19","r21","r22","r23","r24"],"takeaways":["Use the usage counters for totals and cost, the per-operation histograms for outliers, and remember that cached and reasoning tokens are subsets, not extras.","Gateway metric dimensions have hard cardinality limits and excess is dropped silently, so attribute by team or product and read per-user detail from traces.","Pick SLIs for runs, latency, streaming, tools, runaway calls, cost and quality, set targets from your baseline, and alert on metrics.","Choose the backend by ecosystem, stage and what the tool must offer. Instrument once with standard OTLP and mind transport details: Langfuse needs HTTP and LangSmith wants a base endpoint."]},{"id":"w15-l5","order":5,"title":"Boss: make the crew debuggable without leaking data","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer describes a multi-agent system that nobody can debug, with a compliance rule against customer text in logs. The dials are not given. Clarify them, compare the options, choose, and say what would flip your answer.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The prompt and the five steps","level":2,"id":"the-prompt-and-the-five-steps"},{"t":"scenario","title":"What you know and what to ask","known":["A gateway, an orchestrator agent, specialist agents, MCP tools and one A2A peer take part in each request","When an answer is wrong nobody can say which hop or which step caused it","Compliance says customer text must not sit in logs","Finance wants cost per team and on-call wants alerts they can trust"],"ask":["Which data classes pass through prompts and tool results, and what do rules and contracts say about retention and access?","Who must read content when debugging: on-call, support, or only a cleared group?","Which hops are yours, which are hosted tools, and which belong to another team?","What volume and budget are we talking about: traces per second, retention, ingestion cap?","What does wrong mean to the business, and how quickly must on-call respond?"],"tension":"Debugging wants content on every trace. Compliance wants none of it. Cost wants fewer traces, and fewer traces mean the one you need is missing."},{"t":"table","caption":"Dial-and-Defend applied to this question","head":["Step","What you say"],"rows":[["1 Clarify the dials","Data classes, who may read content, which hops are hosted or external, volume and budget, the definition of wrong"],["2 List the options","Metadata only with replay, content on spans in a restricted workspace, external content store with references, redaction before export"],["3 Pick and name the flips","Metadata and references on spans, content in a governed store, trace context tested on every hop. Flip on sensitivity, access rules and platform capacity"],["4 Failure modes and detection","Content bypasses, duplicated content, broken hops, sampled totals, renamed metrics, cardinality drops. Detect with canaries, completeness checks and a name inventory"],["5 Metrics and rollout","SLIs on metrics, tail sampling for errors, pre-production first, a pinned conventions commit"]],"highlightCol":1},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"The sentence that earns points","md":"Say that sampling is a cost control and not a privacy control, and that you would prove privacy with a canary and not by reading flags. Then say the standard is in Development status and moved repositories on 2026-05-05, so you pin it and check which names your build emits."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-l5-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"q":"Compliance says no customer text in logs, yet engineers must debug wrong answers. Which design fits best?","options":["Capture content on spans in production but sample only 1 percent of traces, on the theory that fewer spans mean less exposure and an easier audit","Capture content on spans and mask it in the dashboard layer so viewers do not see it","Turn telemetry off for requests that contain personal data and rely on user complaints","Metadata and references on spans by default, with content in an access-controlled store that has its own retention and audit"],"answer":3,"explain":"The spec recommends external storage with references for production, so debuggers can follow a reference with the right permission while traces stay clean. Sampling limits volume but each retained span still holds customer text, masking in the viewer leaves the stored data unchanged, and turning telemetry off removes the evidence you need.","ref":"r4"},{"id":"w15-l5-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":3,"q":"You need one trace that crosses the gateway, the orchestrator, an MCP tool and an A2A peer. Select every step that helps.","options":["Use client-opened MCP transports for the tools you need traced end to end","Test each hop with a known `traceparent` and look for its trace id in the next service's spans","Log `gen_ai.conversation.id`, task ids and correlation ids so a broken hop can still be joined","Assume the A2A protocol carries trace fields of its own","Rely on head sampling to keep every error trace"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Hosted MCP tools are invoked by the provider and get no injected context, so a client-opened transport restores the chain. A known `traceparent` proves each hop, and logged ids join what propagation cannot. The A2A spec defines no tracing fields, and head sampling cannot guarantee error traces.","ref":"r9"},{"id":"w15-l5-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"q":"After enabling sensitive data, .NET traces show every prompt twice and span storage doubled. What is the likely cause?","options":["Head sampling is retaining each trace twice, because the ratio is applied separately by the client and the agent layers","Both the chat client and the agent are wrapped with OpenTelemetry, so content is recorded on both spans","The OTLP exporter retries failed batches and writes duplicates","The `@trace_function` decorator copies content onto the parent span"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Agent Framework docs warn that instrumenting both layers duplicates prompts and responses on both spans when sensitive data is on, so pick one layer. Sampling does not duplicate traces, exporter retries do not create two spans with different parents, and the decorator records a function's own parameters and return value.","ref":"r10"},{"id":"w15-l5-q4","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"q":"Put the rollout in a defensible order.","items":["Switch on metadata-only tracing with an exporter and confirm spans arrive","Test propagation across every hop with a known `traceparent`","Alert on metrics: error ratio, runaway calls per run, time to first chunk","Add the external content store with access control, retention and a canary leak test","Score a sampled share of live runs and watch the pass rate"],"explain":"Start safe: metadata only. Then prove connectivity, because alerts and debugging need whole traces. Alerts on unsampled metrics come next, because they need no content. The governed content store follows, once the base is trusted and a canary can prove it does not leak. Quality scoring comes last, because it needs both traces and content access.","ref":"r4"}],"defend":{"prompt":"We run a multi-agent system: a gateway, an orchestrator agent, specialist agents, MCP tools and one A2A peer. When an answer is wrong, nobody can say why, and compliance says no customer text may sit in our logs. Make it debuggable without leaking data.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Which data classes pass through prompts and tool results, and what do rules and contracts say about retention and access?","Who must read content when debugging: on-call, support, or only a cleared group?","Which hops are ours, which are hosted tools, and which belong to another team?","What are the volume and the budget: traces per second, retention, an ingestion cap?","What does wrong mean to the business, and how quickly must on-call respond?"],"options":[{"name":"Metadata-only traces plus replay of reproducible cases","when":"Debugging is rare and privacy is strict.","tradeoff":"You cannot read the failing prompt, and replay may not reproduce a nondeterministic failure."},{"name":"Content on spans in a restricted workspace","when":"Data is sandboxed and the same people may see traces and user data.","tradeoff":"Content reaches every trace consumer and retention tier, and operators read user data."},{"name":"Metadata on spans, content in an external store with references","when":"Production data is regulated and debugging is frequent.","tradeoff":"You design the reference convention, the store and the access flow, because the spec still lists external references as TODO."},{"name":"Redaction before export, content on spans","when":"Prompts must stay readable and no platform team can run a store.","tradeoff":"A missed pattern leaks into every consumer, and processing adds cost on every span."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**1. Clarify the dials.** First I would ask about data classes and the rules on retention and access, who must read content when debugging, which hops are ours or hosted or external, volume and budget, and what wrong means to the business."},{"t":"table","head":["Option","Pick when","Loses when"],"rows":[["Metadata only, replay","Rare debugging, strict privacy","You must read the failing prompt"],["Content on spans, restricted workspace","Sandboxed data, same audience","Operators must not see user data"],["External store with references","Regulated data, frequent debugging, a platform to build on","A small team cannot run the store"],["Redaction then spans","Readable prompts, no platform team","A detector miss is unacceptable"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**3. Pick and name the flips.** I would record metadata on every span, keep `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` false in production and put content in a separate store, referenced from spans, with its own access control, retention and audit. The spec recommends this for production [[r4]]. I would wrap one layer per runtime, use client-opened MCP transports where I need the chain [[r10]], and test the A2A hop with a known `traceparent` because the A2A spec has no tracing fields [[r9]]. For volume I would use tail sampling in a collector to keep errors and slow runs, and alert from metrics, which are not sampled [[r15]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w15-d3","title":"The shape I would defend","alt":"Agent services send spans through a collector with tail sampling to a trace backend, send metrics to a metrics and alerts backend, and write content to a separate content store with access control and expiry. Traces refer to the content store.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Agent services] --> C[Collector with tail sampling]\n  C --> T[Trace backend]\n  A --> K[Metrics and alerts]\n  A --> V[Content store with RBAC and TTL]\n  T -.-> V","caption":"Spans carry references. Only the content store holds text, and only a cleared group opens it."},{"t":"p","md":"Flips: low sensitivity moves me to content on spans. If operators may see user data, a restricted workspace with content on spans is simpler. If no platform team exists, I would capture full content only in pre-production with synthetic data and reproduce production failures there, or accept redaction with its miss rate."},{"t":"p","md":"**4. Failure modes and detection.** Content can bypass the flags through `@trace_function` and hand-written attributes, so I run a canary and search the backend [[r13]]. Wrapping both .NET layers duplicates content. Hosted MCP tools break the chain. Sums over sampled spans come out low unless weighted by `itemCount`. The conventions are in Development and moved repositories on 2026-05-05, so metric names can change [[r1]] [[r5]]. Per-user metric dimensions are dropped silently past the limits [[r16]]. I detect these with a trace completeness check (the share of traces with spans from every service), a retained-percentage query and a CI inventory of emitted metric names."},{"t":"p","md":"**5. Metrics and rollout.** SLIs are run availability, p95 run duration without human pauses, p95 first-chunk time, tool error ratio, p99 calls per run, tokens per successful task and an online quality pass rate, with targets taken from a baseline. Rollout: metadata-only tracing, propagation tests, alerts, the content store with a canary leak test, then quality scoring. I would pin the conventions by commit and review it before every upgrade."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Clarifies data classes, who may read content, which hops are hosted or external, volume and the definition of wrong before choosing","w":3},{"pt":"Separates metadata from content and picks an external store with references for production, with access control, retention and audit","w":3},{"pt":"Names which flag controls which layer and the bypasses (`@trace_function`, custom attributes, duplicate wrapping), and proves privacy with a canary","w":3},{"pt":"Explains propagation over HTTP, MCP `params._meta` and A2A, including the hosted MCP gap and testing the A2A hop","w":2},{"pt":"Chooses sampling deliberately: head versus tail, metrics unsampled for alerts, `itemCount` weighting for sums","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with detection and gives SLIs with targets taken from a baseline","w":2},{"pt":"States the Development status of the conventions, pins them and checks the emitted names; picks a backend by constraint","w":2},{"pt":"States flip conditions: sensitivity, who may see data, platform capacity","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"Compliance asks you to prove that no customer text reached the backend.","a":"Run a canary: a unique marker in test prompts, tool arguments and tool results on every path, then search the backend and the content store for it. Check the flag of every layer (`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA`, `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT`) and review every `@trace_function`. Repeat after each dependency upgrade [[r10]] [[r13]]."},{"q":"A load test shows about half the traces you expect.","a":"The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro default is rate-limited sampling at five traces per second, so bursts are dropped. Use a fixed ratio or a higher rate for tests, check the retained percentage with the `itemCount` query, and count requests from metrics, which are not sampled [[r15]]."},{"q":"A metric name changes next quarter. How do you protect the dashboards?","a":"The conventions are in Development and have already changed repositories and metric names. Pin the conventions by commit, keep an inventory of emitted metric names that CI compares with dashboard queries, and alert on metrics you control [[r1]] [[r5]]."}],"redFlags":["Turns on content capture everywhere to make debugging easy.","Treats sampling as a privacy control.","Assumes trace context crosses hosted MCP tools or an A2A hop without testing it.","Computes cost by summing sampled spans or by adding cached tokens to input tokens.","Builds dashboards on metric names without pinning the conventions version."]},"refs":["r1","r4","r5","r9","r10","r13","r15","r16"],"takeaways":["Debuggable and private means metadata on every span, content in a governed store behind references, and trace context tested on every hop.","Sampling is a cost control, not a privacy control. Prove privacy with a canary, and check the flag of every layer plus the bypasses.","Alert on metrics because they are not sampled, keep errors with tail sampling, and weight sampled sums by `itemCount`.","The conventions are in Development status. Pin them, check which names your build emits, and say so in the interview."]}],"topics":[{"id":"w15-t01","order":1,"group":"Foundations","title":"What to observe in LLM systems","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"LLM systems fail quietly: the request succeeds, the text is fluent and the content is wrong. Observability records the shape of every run (spans, tokens, finish reasons, tool calls) and treats content as a separate, governed decision.","useIf":"You operate, or are about to operate, a model-backed feature and need to explain wrong answers, slow runs and surprising bills.","avoidIf":"You expect status codes and error rates alone to tell you whether the system works.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A classic service fails loudly with a 500, a timeout or an exception. An LLM system usually returns 200. Error rates stay flat while answer quality drops, cost scales with tokens, latency depends on how many model and tool calls a run made, and the evidence you need to debug (the prompt, the retrieved text, the tool result) is also the data you must protect. That is why observability here has more jobs than uptime."},{"t":"table","caption":"What changes compared with a classic service","head":["Question","Classic service","LLM or agent system","Signal that answers it"],"rows":[["Did it fail?","Status code, exception","Often no: 200 with a wrong answer","Online quality scores, user feedback"],["Why was it slow?","One request path","Several model and tool calls per run, plus waits","Nested spans, `gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls` and `.tool_calls`"],["What did it cost?","Per request, stable","Per token, varies with context, cache and reasoning","Token usage counters by model and agent"],["What happened inside?","Logs and stack traces","Prompts, retrieved chunks, tool arguments and results","Opt-in content capture, held outside the span"],["Is it drifting?","Rare","Model, prompt or data changes shift quality silently","Sampled online evaluation, scheduled test sets"]]},{"t":"p","md":"The OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions give a shared vocabulary for the shape: spans such as `chat`, `invoke_agent` and `execute_tool`, token and duration metrics, and opt-in content attributes. All of them carry the status **Development** as of 2026-10-13, so pin versions and expect renames [[r1]]. Agent Framework emits these spans and metrics for you once telemetry is on [[r10]], and Foundry can collect traces server-side into Application Insights [[r12]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Shape first, content second","md":"Spans, token counts, finish reasons and tool-call counts rarely contain personal data and answer most operational questions. Prompts and outputs answer the rest but need consent, redaction, retention limits and access control. Decide them separately (see content capture, redaction and privacy)."}],"whereToUse":["A support assistant whose answers are fluent but sometimes wrong and nobody can say why","An agent platform where one user request fans out into many model and tool calls","A cost review after a token bill rose without a matching traffic increase","A release gate that needs to know whether a prompt change moved quality or latency","An incident where a downstream tool or MCP server failed silently inside an agent run"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Trace every agent run end to end with model, tool and agent spans before launch","Emit token and duration metrics, which are not sampled, for dashboards and alerts","Score a sample of live traffic for quality so silent failures become visible"],"avoid":["Do not rely on HTTP error rates as the health signal for answer quality","Do not capture prompts and outputs everywhere by default","Do not build dashboards on metric names you have not checked against what your build emits"],"flips":["Data is regulated: keep shape signals in the main pipeline and move content to a separate, access-controlled store","Traffic is tiny and non-critical: console or local traces may be enough until you launch","Quality matters more than uptime for the feature: invest in online scoring before extra infrastructure dashboards"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to find out what happened","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Traces and metrics (this chapter)","You need run structure, latency and cost per step","You need to read the actual text","Needs instrumentation and a backend"],["Application logs of prompts and outputs","A prototype with synthetic data","Real users, regulated data or high volume","Leaks personal data, hard to correlate across hops"],["Periodic offline evaluation only","Release gating on a golden set","Live drift and unseen inputs","Blind between test runs"],["User feedback only","Cheap signal of real dissatisfaction","Silent errors that users do not notice","Sparse, biased and slow"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The answer was wrong, yet every span is green and the error rate is flat","cause":"Only transport status was monitored, and quality is a semantic property","fix":"Add run-level attributes (finish reasons, tool-call counts) and sampled online quality scores"},{"symptom":"A cost panel disagrees with the provider bill","cause":"Cached and reasoning tokens were added on top of input and output totals, or sampled spans were summed","fix":"Treat cached and reasoning counts as subsets and build totals from unsampled counters [[r5]]"},{"symptom":"Debugging requires prompts that nobody is allowed to store","cause":"Content capture was an all-or-nothing switch","fix":"Store content in a governed external store and keep references on spans [[r4]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t01-s1","title":"Fluent but wrong, and no way to say why","situation":"A support assistant sometimes quotes the wrong policy. Error rates and latency are normal. The team has only HTTP logs. Customer data is involved and legal has not approved storing prompts.","constraints":["Personal data in prompts","No approval to store content yet","Quality problem, not an outage"],"unknowns":["How many model and tool calls does a run make?","Is retrieval the suspect or the model?","Can a restricted store hold content later?"],"question":"What do you add first?","options":[{"name":"Run traces with agent, model, retrieval and tool spans plus finish reasons and token counts, content off","verdict":"best","why":"It shows which step ran, how long it took and whether retrieval returned anything, without storing text.","tradeoff":"You cannot read the wrong answer itself until a governed content store is approved."},{"name":"Log full prompts and answers to the application log","verdict":"poor","why":"It stores customer data without approval and is hard to correlate across steps.","tradeoff":"Fine for a lab with synthetic data."},{"name":"Add a weekly offline evaluation run","verdict":"good","why":"It measures quality on a golden set and catches regressions.","tradeoff":"It does not explain live failures or see unseen inputs."}],"flips":[{"if":"Legal approves a restricted store with short retention","then":"Add content capture by reference for sampled and failed runs."},{"if":"The data is synthetic or internal only","then":"Capture content on spans in non-production."}],"takeaway":"Start with run shape, which is safe and answers most questions."},{"id":"w15-t01-s2","title":"The bill doubled while traffic did not","situation":"Monthly token spend doubled. Request volume grew 10 percent. Three teams share one model deployment through a gateway.","constraints":["Shared deployment","Spend moved, traffic did not","Need attribution by team and agent"],"unknowns":["Did context length or reasoning usage grow?","Did a cache stop hitting?","Which agent loops more?"],"question":"Which signal do you build first?","options":[{"name":"Token usage counters by model and agent, plus inference-call counts per run","verdict":"best","why":"Counters are unsampled and show whether tokens per call or calls per run rose.","tradeoff":"Needs careful dimension choices to avoid cardinality blowups."},{"name":"Sampled trace review","verdict":"good","why":"Good for explaining a spike once you know where to look.","tradeoff":"Sampling distorts totals."},{"name":"Request-count dashboard","verdict":"poor","why":"Requests barely changed, so it cannot explain the spike.","tradeoff":"Useful as a denominator for tokens per request."}],"flips":[{"if":"Teams share a gateway","then":"Emit token metrics at the gateway with a team dimension."},{"if":"One agent shows rising calls per run","then":"Inspect its traces for a loop or retry storm."}],"takeaway":"Use unsampled counters for totals and traces for explanations."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Why can an LLM feature be broken while its error rate and latency look healthy?","options":["The request returns success even when the answer is wrong, so quality needs its own signals","LLM calls never raise exceptions","Latency does not matter for LLM systems","Token counts hide the error rate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Transport success says nothing about semantic correctness, so you need online quality scoring and run-level signals. LLM clients do raise errors, latency does matter and token counts are unrelated to error rates."},{"id":"w15-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which signals are generally safe to collect for every run without storing user content? Select all that apply.","options":["Token usage counts and finish reasons","Tool-call counts and durations","Full prompt and response text on every span","Agent, model and tool span names and durations"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Shape signals (counts, durations, names, finish reasons) rarely contain personal data. Full prompts and responses are the sensitive part and are opt-in under the GenAI conventions."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is HTTP success not enough for an LLM feature?","back":"A 200 response can carry a fluent but wrong answer. You need run-level signals and sampled quality scoring as well."},{"front":"Which signals describe the shape of a run?","back":"Spans for agent, model and tool steps, token usage, durations, finish reasons and tool-call counts."},{"front":"What is the status of the OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions as of 2026-10-13?","back":"Development. They moved to the semantic-conventions-genai repository on 2026-05-05, so pin versions and expect renames."}],"related":["w15-t02","w15-t03","w15-t06","w15-t11","w14-t01","w14-t02"],"refs":["r1","r4","r5","r10","r12"]},{"id":"w15-t02","order":2,"group":"Standards","title":"OTel GenAI semantic conventions","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions define shared span names, attributes and metrics for model calls, agents, tools, workflows and MCP, so any instrumented app can feed any compatible backend. All of it has status Development.","useIf":"You instrument LLM or agent code and want dashboards, alerts and backends that survive a change of framework or vendor.","avoidIf":"You treat attribute and metric names as frozen: they are still changing and moved repositories on 2026-05-05.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The conventions cover client inference spans and metrics, model spans, agent and framework spans, events, exceptions, token metrics and MCP. Every part carries the status **Development** (checked 2026-10-13). The docs moved from `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions` to `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai` (PR 3696, merged 2026-05-05). Release v1.41.1 (2026-05-11) of the old repository still holds the full text, v1.42.0 (2026-06-12) holds only a Moved stub, and opentelemetry.io shows Moved. The new repository has no tags or releases, so pin by commit [[r1]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Attribute families you will meet [[r2]] [[r3]]","head":["Family","Examples","Use it for"],"rows":[["Operation and provider","`gen_ai.operation.name` (`chat`, `embeddings`, `retrieval`, `execute_tool`, `invoke_agent`, `invoke_workflow`, `plan`), `gen_ai.provider.name`","Filtering by kind of work and vendor"],["Request and response","`gen_ai.request.model`, `gen_ai.request.temperature`, `gen_ai.request.max_tokens`, `gen_ai.response.finish_reasons`, `gen_ai.response.time_to_first_chunk`","Reproducing a call, spotting truncation"],["Usage","`gen_ai.usage.input_tokens`, `.output_tokens`, `.cache_read.input_tokens`, `.cache_write.input_tokens`, `.reasoning.output_tokens`","Cost and cache analysis"],["Agent and conversation","`gen_ai.agent.id`, `gen_ai.agent.name`, `gen_ai.conversation.id`","Grouping runs by agent and session"],["Content (opt-in)","`gen_ai.input.messages`, `gen_ai.output.messages`, `gen_ai.system_instructions`, `gen_ai.tool.call.arguments`, `gen_ai.tool.call.result`","Debugging, under a privacy policy [[r4]]"],["Errors","`error.type`","Failure rates per operation"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Required on inference spans are `gen_ai.operation.name` and `gen_ai.provider.name`. Metric names have churned: the v1.41 text defined a `gen_ai.client.token.usage` histogram that no longer appears in the new repository, which defines token **counters** (`gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*`) instead, while Agent Framework's docs still list `gen_ai.client.token.usage` [[r5]] [[r10]]. Check what your build and backend emit before you build dashboards."},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Development status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Older v1.41.1 text said instrumentations that emitted v1.36.0 or earlier must not change their defaults and should offer `OTEL_SEMCONV_STABILITY_OPT_IN=gen_ai_latest_experimental`. The new docs do not repeat this, so whether instrumentations still honor it is UNVERIFIED."}],"whereToUse":["Choosing attribute names for custom spans around model, retrieval and tool calls","Writing dashboards and alerts that should work across Agent Framework, other SDKs and gateways","Evaluating whether a backend understands GenAI traces without custom mapping","Setting an organization-wide telemetry standard for many teams","Reviewing vendor claims about OpenTelemetry compatibility"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Adopt the GenAI names for spans, operations and token usage in new instrumentation","Wrap them in one small internal module so a rename touches one place","Pin the convention commit or library version and record it in your runbook"],"avoid":["Do not hard-code metric names in alerts without confirming what the build emits","Do not invent parallel attribute names for things the conventions already name","Do not enable content attributes by default"],"flips":["A backend only understands its own schema: keep the OTel names and rely on its mapping, or map in a collector","You need stability for a regulated audit trail: record the convention version with each release","Your framework already emits the conventions: do not re-instrument by hand"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Naming schemes for GenAI telemetry","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["OTel GenAI conventions","You want portable telemetry across frameworks and backends","You need guaranteed stability today","Status Development, renames and repository moves [[r1]]"],["OpenInference (Phoenix ecosystem)","You use Phoenix and its auto-instrumentation","Your other tools expect `gen_ai.*`","A second vocabulary to map [[r19]]"],["Vendor SDK attributes","A single vendor backend with rich features","You may switch vendors","Lock-in to attribute names"],["Custom names only","A narrow, internal-only need","Any shared tooling","No ecosystem support, every dashboard is bespoke"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A token panel built on `gen_ai.client.token.usage` goes blank after an upgrade","cause":"Metric names changed: newer conventions use `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` counters","fix":"Inspect the names your build emits, pin versions and keep dashboard queries in one mapping layer [[r5]]"},{"symptom":"Documentation links lead to a Moved page","cause":"The conventions moved repositories on 2026-05-05 and some docs still link the old URL","fix":"Cite and pin the semantic-conventions-genai repository by commit [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Traces show up in a backend but cost and agent views stay empty","cause":"The backend expects attribute names your instrumentation does not set, such as `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens`","fix":"Verify with a query on a few spans, then add the missing attributes or map them in a collector [[r14]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t02-s1","title":"Six teams, two backends, one telemetry standard","situation":"Six teams build agents on different frameworks. Two backends are in use. A platform team wants shared dashboards and alerts.","constraints":["Mixed frameworks","Two backends","Conventions still in Development"],"unknowns":["Which frameworks already emit GenAI spans?","Which backends map OTel attributes natively?"],"question":"What standard do you set?","options":[{"name":"Adopt the OTel GenAI names, pin a version and wrap custom spans in one internal helper","verdict":"best","why":"Shared names make dashboards portable and the helper absorbs renames.","tradeoff":"You must track the repository and test upgrades."},{"name":"Let each team choose its own attribute names","verdict":"poor","why":"No shared dashboard or alert can work across teams.","tradeoff":"Only acceptable for throwaway prototypes."},{"name":"Adopt one vendor SDK's attributes for everyone","verdict":"good","why":"Fast to roll out with one backend's features.","tradeoff":"Lock-in and poor fit for the second backend."}],"flips":[{"if":"Compliance requires a frozen schema","then":"Snapshot the convention version and run a collector transform to a stable internal schema."},{"if":"Only one team and one backend exist","then":"The framework's defaults are enough."}],"takeaway":"Standardize on shared names and isolate change in one place."},{"id":"w15-t02-s2","title":"A dashboard goes blank after a framework upgrade","situation":"A token usage dashboard stopped showing data the day a framework was upgraded. Traces still arrive.","constraints":["Traces arrive","Metric series vanished","Upgrade is the only change"],"unknowns":["Which metric names does the new build emit?","Do other panels use the same names?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"List the emitted metric names, update the queries and keep names in one mapping layer","verdict":"best","why":"It fixes the cause and prevents repeats when names change again.","tradeoff":"A small amount of indirection to maintain."},{"name":"Roll back the framework","verdict":"good","why":"Restores the panel quickly.","tradeoff":"Delays the upgrade and the next one breaks it again."},{"name":"Assume the metrics are lost and rebuild the dashboard from traces","verdict":"poor","why":"Traces are sampled, so totals will be wrong.","tradeoff":"Traces help explain a spike but are not a source for totals."}],"flips":[{"if":"The new names are documented and stable","then":"Update once and move on."},{"if":"Both old and new builds run together","then":"Query both names during the migration."}],"takeaway":"Verify what is emitted before trusting a dashboard."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the status of the OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions as of 2026-10-13?","options":["Stable","Development","Release candidate","Deprecated"],"answer":1,"explain":"All GenAI conventions carry the status Development. They moved to the semantic-conventions-genai repository on 2026-05-05 and have no tagged release yet, so names can change."},{"id":"w15-t02-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each attribute to the question it helps answer.","pairs":[{"l":"`gen_ai.operation.name`","r":"Is this a chat, tool or agent step?"},{"l":"`gen_ai.response.finish_reasons`","r":"Was the output truncated?"},{"l":"`gen_ai.usage.cache_read.input_tokens`","r":"How much input hit the cache?"},{"l":"`gen_ai.conversation.id`","r":"Which session did this run belong to?"}],"explain":"Operation name classifies the work, finish reasons reveal truncation, cache-read tokens measure caching and the conversation id groups runs."},{"id":"w15-t02-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"After an upgrade, a panel based on `gen_ai.client.token.usage` is empty although traces arrive. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Sampling removed all the data","The backend dropped traces older than a day","The build emits different metric names, such as token counters under `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*`","Content capture was turned on"],"answer":2,"explain":"Metric names differ between convention versions and builds. Metrics are not sampled, retention does not explain a blank live panel and content capture does not affect metric names."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Where do the OTel GenAI conventions live since 2026-05-05?","back":"In the `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai` repository. It has no tagged release, so pin by commit."},{"front":"Which two attributes are required on inference spans?","back":"`gen_ai.operation.name` and `gen_ai.provider.name`."},{"front":"Why are metric names risky to hard-code?","back":"Older text defined a `gen_ai.client.token.usage` histogram. The new repository uses `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` counters, so builds differ."}],"related":["w15-t01","w15-t03","w15-t04","w15-t12","w11-t26"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5","r10","r14","r19"]},{"id":"w15-t03","order":3,"group":"Standards","title":"Spans for chat, agent and tool calls","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An agent run becomes a tree of spans: invoke_agent at the top, a chat span for each model call and an execute_tool span for each tool call, optionally under an invoke_workflow span. The nesting shows where time and tokens went.","useIf":"You need to see which model calls, tool calls and sub-agents made up one agent run and how long each took.","avoidIf":"You only need aggregate rates: metrics are cheaper and unsampled for that.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The GenAI conventions name spans by operation. An inference span is `{gen_ai.operation.name} {gen_ai.request.model}` (for example `chat my-model`), kind CLIENT, or INTERNAL for in-process models. An agent run is `invoke_agent {gen_ai.agent.name}`, CLIENT for a remote agent service and INTERNAL for an in-process framework. A tool call is `execute_tool {gen_ai.tool.name}`, INTERNAL. A graph or orchestrator that coordinates several agents is `invoke_workflow {gen_ai.workflow.name}`, INTERNAL. There are also `create_agent` (CLIENT) and `plan` (INTERNAL) spans [[r3]] [[r2]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w15-t03-d1","title":"A traced run: workflow, agent, model and tool spans","alt":"A workflow span contains an agent span. The agent span contains two chat spans and an execute tool span. The execute tool span contains an MCP tools call span.","src":"flowchart TD\n  W[invoke_workflow review] --> A[invoke_agent planner]\n  A --> C1[chat model first call]\n  A --> T[execute_tool search_docs]\n  T --> M[tools/call search_docs]\n  A --> C2[chat model second call]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Names, kinds and what to record [[r3]] [[r6]]","head":["Operation","Span name","Kind","Record at least"],"rows":[["Model call","`chat {model}`","CLIENT","Provider, model, finish reasons, token usage, `error.type`"],["Agent run","`invoke_agent {agent}`","INTERNAL or CLIENT","Agent name and id, conversation id, run duration"],["Workflow run","`invoke_workflow {workflow}`","INTERNAL","Workflow name, total duration, error"],["Tool call","`execute_tool {tool}`","INTERNAL","Tool name, duration, error (arguments and results are opt-in)"],["MCP request","`{mcp.method.name} {target}`, for example `tools/call get-weather`","CLIENT and SERVER","Method, target, duration"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Agent Framework emits `invoke_agent <agent_name>`, `chat <model_name>` and `execute_tool <function_name>` [[r10]]. For workflows it also emits `workflow.build`, `workflow.session` or `workflow.run`, `executor.process {executor_id}`, `edge_group.process` and `message.send` spans. The OTel `invoke_workflow` span is newer than that doc page (dated 2026-05-27), so whether current builds emit it is UNVERIFIED [[r11]]. In .NET the `gen_ai.*` spans appear only after you wrap the client or agent with `UseOpenTelemetry`, and wrapping both layers duplicates content when sensitive data is on [[r10]]."}],"whereToUse":["Finding which of several model calls or tools made an agent run slow","Spotting loops: many `chat` and `execute_tool` spans under one `invoke_agent`","Seeing which executor or branch of a workflow blocked a superstep","Checking that a tool or MCP server was called with the expected duration and error","Building per-agent and per-tool latency and error dashboards from span attributes"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Trace agent, model and tool spans for every production agent run","Add workflow spans when several agents or executors cooperate","Keep span names low-cardinality: operation plus model, agent or tool name"],"avoid":["Do not put user text or ids into span names","Do not wrap both the chat client and the agent in .NET with content capture on","Do not trace every token chunk as a span"],"flips":["Volume is huge and cost matters: keep span structure and sample traces, use metrics for totals","You call a remote agent service: record it as a CLIENT `invoke_agent` and propagate context","A framework already emits these spans: add only business-level spans of your own"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Granularity of instrumentation","head":["Level","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["One span per request","Cheap overview of latency and errors","A run makes many model and tool calls","You cannot tell which step was slow"],["Model-call spans only (provider SDK instrumentation)","Cost and latency per call","You need to group calls by agent or tool","No run-level structure"],["Agent, chat and tool spans (this topic)","Debugging runs and loops","Volume is extreme","More spans, so sampling and storage cost"],["Add workflow and executor spans","Multi-agent graphs and parallel branches","Single-agent apps","Extra spans to maintain and name consistently"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A .NET service emits no `gen_ai.*` spans at all","cause":"The client or agent was not wrapped with the OpenTelemetry delegating wrapper, or the provider has no matching `AddSource`","fix":"Wrap the agent or client once with `UseOpenTelemetry` and call `AddSource` with the same source name [[r10]]"},{"symptom":"Prompts appear twice on parent and child spans","cause":"Both the chat client and the agent were instrumented with sensitive data on","fix":"Instrument one layer and keep the other uninstrumented [[r10]]"},{"symptom":"A run shows dozens of `chat` spans under one `invoke_agent`","cause":"A tool loop or retry cycle without a stop condition","fix":"Alert on calls per run (`gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls`) and set loop and budget limits"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t03-s1","title":"A new agent with four tools and one MCP server","situation":"A team launches an agent that calls four local tools and one MCP server. They want to debug slow or wrong runs from day one. Data is internal and low volume.","constraints":["Small team, low volume","Internal data","Launch in two weeks"],"unknowns":["Does the framework emit GenAI spans already?","Will the MCP calls cross a trace boundary?"],"question":"What do you require before launch?","options":[{"name":"Framework telemetry for agent, chat and tool spans, an exporter, and context propagation to the MCP server","verdict":"best","why":"It gives the full run tree at low effort and shows tool and MCP latency inside the run.","tradeoff":"You must pick an exporter and a backend and verify the spans arrive."},{"name":"Provider-SDK model spans only","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and shows model latency and tokens.","tradeoff":"No grouping by agent or tool, so loops and slow tools stay hidden."},{"name":"Log lines around each call","verdict":"poor","why":"Logs do not give parent-child timing or cross-service correlation.","tradeoff":"Fine for a one-off script."}],"flips":[{"if":"The MCP server is a provider-hosted tool","then":"Expect a gap in propagation and correlate by call ids."},{"if":"Volume grows by 100 times","then":"Keep the spans and add sampling."}],"takeaway":"A nested run tree answers most why-was-it-slow questions."},{"id":"w15-t03-s2","title":"A workflow where one branch is slow","situation":"A workflow runs three agents in parallel inside each step and then merges results. Total latency is often twice the average agent time.","constraints":["Parallel branches","Barrier between steps","Need to find the straggler"],"unknowns":["Which executor is slowest per step?","Is the slow branch a model, tool or retry?"],"question":"How do you find the bottleneck?","options":[{"name":"Workflow and executor spans with nested agent, chat and tool spans","verdict":"best","why":"The slowest executor in each step and the span below it are visible in one trace.","tradeoff":"More spans per run and extra storage."},{"name":"Per-agent average latency metrics","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and unsampled for trends.","tradeoff":"Averages hide the straggler that blocks each step."},{"name":"HTTP server spans only","verdict":"poor","why":"They show total time but not the structure of the run.","tradeoff":"Fine for classic request paths."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only one agent exists","then":"Agent, chat and tool spans are enough."},{"if":"Spans are too costly","then":"Sample traces and alert on p95 from metrics."}],"takeaway":"Parallel work needs per-executor spans to find the straggler."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t03-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order these spans from outermost parent to innermost child for a workflow that calls an agent, which calls an MCP-backed tool.","items":["`invoke_workflow review`","`invoke_agent planner`","`execute_tool search_docs`","`tools/call search_docs`"],"explain":"The workflow coordinates agents, the agent runs the tool, and the tool call becomes the MCP request. The MCP client span is a child of the tool span."},{"id":"w15-t03-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each span name pattern to the work it describes.","pairs":[{"l":"`chat {model}`","r":"One model call"},{"l":"`invoke_agent {agent}`","r":"One agent run"},{"l":"`execute_tool {tool}`","r":"One tool call"},{"l":"`invoke_workflow {workflow}`","r":"A graph that coordinates several agents"}],"explain":"The first token of each name is the operation and the second identifies the model, agent, tool or workflow."},{"id":"w15-t03-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A .NET agent runs fine but no `gen_ai.*` spans show up in the backend. What should you check first?","options":["That the agent or chat client is wrapped with `UseOpenTelemetry` and the tracer provider calls `AddSource` with the same name","That the model deployment supports tracing","That sampling is set to 100 percent","That the Agent Card is signed"],"answer":0,"explain":"In .NET the spans appear only after wrapping, and the provider must listen to the same source name. Models do not control tracing, sampling cannot create spans that do not exist and Agent Cards are unrelated."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the span names for a model call, an agent run and a tool call?","back":"`chat {model}`, `invoke_agent {agent}` and `execute_tool {tool}`. Workflows use `invoke_workflow {workflow}`."},{"front":"Which span kinds do these operations use?","back":"Inference is CLIENT (INTERNAL for in-process models), `invoke_agent` is CLIENT for remote services and INTERNAL in-process, `execute_tool` and `invoke_workflow` are INTERNAL."},{"front":"Why can .NET show no `gen_ai.*` spans?","back":"The agent or client was not wrapped with `UseOpenTelemetry`, or the provider did not `AddSource` the same name. The Harness wraps both layers by default."}],"related":["w15-t02","w15-t05","w15-t09","w11-t14","w11-t26","w10-t10"],"refs":["r2","r3","r6","r10","r11"]},{"id":"w15-t04","order":4,"group":"Standards","title":"Metrics: tokens, duration and cost","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Metrics are unsampled aggregates. Use token usage counters for totals and cost, duration histograms for latency and agent metrics for loops, and keep dimensions low-cardinality so attribution does not break the backend.","useIf":"You need trustworthy totals for tokens, latency and cost, dashboards and alerts that sampling must not distort.","avoidIf":"You compute totals from sampled spans or per-operation histograms: both give wrong numbers.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The GenAI metrics in the new conventions repository split by job [[r5]] [[r2]]. **Counters** carry totals: `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens` (includes cached tokens) and `.output_tokens` (includes reasoning tokens), with subset counters `.cache_read.input_tokens`, `.cache_write.input_tokens` and `.reasoning.output_tokens`, split by `gen_ai.token.modality`. **Histograms** describe distributions: `gen_ai.client.inference.duration`, `.time_to_first_chunk` (streaming only) and `.time_per_output_chunk` use unit seconds and explicit buckets from 0.01 doubling to 81.92 (14 boundaries). The per-operation token histograms `gen_ai.client.inference.operation.input_tokens` and `.output_tokens` are for percentiles and outliers and should not be used for total usage or cost."},{"t":"p","md":"Agent-level metrics include `gen_ai.invoke_agent.duration`, `gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls`, `gen_ai.invoke_agent.tool_calls`, `gen_ai.invoke_workflow.duration` and `gen_ai.execute_tool.duration`. Agent Framework's docs list `gen_ai.client.operation.duration`, `gen_ai.client.token.usage` and `agent_framework.function.invocation.duration`, so confirm which names your build emits [[r10]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Cost from counters","expr":"cost = (input - cache_read) x p_in + cache_read x p_cache + output x p_out","vars":[{"k":"input","v":"gen_ai.client.inference.usage.input_tokens (already includes cache reads)"},{"k":"output","v":"gen_ai.client.inference.usage.output_tokens (already includes reasoning tokens)"},{"k":"p_*","v":"Your price per token for uncached input, cached input and output"}],"example":"Illustrative prices: 2.00 per million uncached input, 0.50 per million cached input, 8.00 per million output. A day with 1,000,000 input tokens (600,000 cache reads) and 100,000 output tokens costs 0.80 + 0.30 + 0.80 = 1.90. Adding cache reads on top of input would report 1,600,000 input tokens and overstate cost."},{"t":"p","md":"To attribute cost at a shared gateway, API Management's `llm-emit-token-metric` policy sends token metrics to Application Insights with up to five custom dimensions. Azure Monitor allows 10 dimension keys per metric and 50,000 active time series per region per subscription (12 hours). API Management allows 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active time series per namespace and **drops the excess silently**. Interrupted streams give wrong counts and some OpenAI streams need `include_usage` set to true. Cached and reasoning categories are in preview [[r16]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Do not use a user id as a dimension","md":"A per-user dimension exceeds 100 unique values quickly, so series vanish without an error. Use team, agent, model or tier on metrics and keep user ids on spans."}],"whereToUse":["A cost dashboard by team, agent and model built from token counters","Latency SLIs from duration histograms, including time to first chunk for streaming","Runaway detection from calls per run and tool calls per run","Gateway-level token metering for chargeback across teams","Cache effectiveness checks from cache-read counters"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Use counters for totals and cost, and alert on metrics because they are not sampled","Use duration histograms with the recommended bucket boundaries for percentiles","Use span attributes for per-request drill-down, not metric dimensions"],"avoid":["Do not add cache or reasoning tokens on top of input and output totals","Do not compute cost from per-operation histograms or sampled spans","Do not put high-cardinality values such as user ids on metrics"],"flips":["You need per-user cost: aggregate from unsampled logs or a billing export, not metric dimensions","Streams are interrupted often: compare gateway counts with provider usage before trusting them","Cached or reasoning categories are in preview in your backend: keep a fallback computed from span attributes"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where token and cost numbers come from","head":["Source","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Client counters (`gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*`)","Per agent and model totals","Calls bypass your instrumented client","Naming differs across builds [[r5]]"],["Gateway metric (`llm-emit-token-metric`)","Many teams behind one gateway","You need per-run detail","Dimension limits drop series silently [[r16]]"],["Sampled span attributes","Explaining one spike or run","Computing totals","Sampling distorts sums unless weighted by `itemCount` [[r15]]"],["Provider billing export","Final invoice reconciliation","Real-time alerting","Delay and coarse attribution"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The cost dashboard shows more tokens than the provider bill","cause":"Cache-read or reasoning tokens were added to input and output totals, but they are subsets","fix":"Compute uncached input as `input - cache_read` and treat reasoning as part of output [[r5]]"},{"symptom":"A per-user cost metric loses series without any error","cause":"The dimension exceeded 100 unique values per dimension in API Management","fix":"Replace it with team or tier and keep user ids on spans [[r16]]"},{"symptom":"Gateway token counts are low for streamed calls","cause":"Interrupted streams never delivered final usage, or the stream did not include usage","fix":"Set `include_usage` where required and reconcile with provider usage [[r16]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t04-s1","title":"Chargeback for twelve teams behind one gateway","situation":"Twelve teams share model deployments through an API gateway. Finance wants monthly cost per team and engineering wants per-agent token trends. Per-user cost is not required.","constraints":["Shared gateway","Per-team attribution","Low-cardinality dimensions"],"unknowns":["Do all calls go through the gateway?","Are streamed calls common?"],"question":"How do you attribute tokens?","options":[{"name":"Gateway token metric with team and agent dimensions, plus client counters for per-agent trends","verdict":"best","why":"Unsampled totals at the gateway cover every team, and low-cardinality dimensions stay within limits.","tradeoff":"Gateway-only counts can be wrong for interrupted streams, so reconcile monthly."},{"name":"Sum token attributes from sampled spans","verdict":"poor","why":"Sampling distorts totals and rare heavy runs are missed.","tradeoff":"Fine for explaining a spike, not for chargeback."},{"name":"Use the provider invoice only","verdict":"good","why":"Accurate in total.","tradeoff":"No per-team split and a monthly delay."}],"flips":[{"if":"Per-user cost is required","then":"Join gateway logs with identity in a warehouse, not metric dimensions."},{"if":"Some calls bypass the gateway","then":"Add client-side counters and reconcile both."}],"takeaway":"Totals come from unsampled counters with low-cardinality labels."},{"id":"w15-t04-s2","title":"A latency SLI for a streaming assistant","situation":"A streaming chat assistant needs a latency SLI that reflects what users feel. Replies stream for 5 to 30 seconds.","constraints":["Streaming responses","User-perceived latency matters","Need percentiles"],"unknowns":["Is time to first token or full duration more important?","Do tool calls precede the first chunk?"],"question":"Which metric do you base the SLI on?","options":[{"name":"`gen_ai.client.inference.time_to_first_chunk` p95, with total duration as a secondary SLI","verdict":"best","why":"First-chunk time is what users feel and it is a histogram with percentile-friendly buckets.","tradeoff":"It exists only for streaming calls and ignores tool time before the first chunk."},{"name":"Average total duration","verdict":"poor","why":"Averages hide the tail and total duration scales with answer length.","tradeoff":"Fine for batch jobs."},{"name":"Per-operation output token histogram","verdict":"good","why":"Reveals outliers in answer length.","tradeoff":"It measures size, not latency."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools run before the first chunk","then":"Add agent-run duration and tool duration SLIs."},{"if":"Responses are not streamed","then":"Use inference duration percentiles."}],"takeaway":"Measure the latency the user perceives, and use percentiles."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"With 8,000 input tokens reported, of which 6,000 are cache reads, how many input tokens were charged at the full uncached price?","answer":2000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"tokens","hint":"Cache reads are a subset of input tokens.","explain":"Uncached input is input minus cache reads: 8,000 - 6,000 = 2,000. Adding the cache reads would wrongly give 14,000."},{"id":"w15-t04-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are sound ways to get token totals for cost? Select all that apply.","options":["Token usage counters, which are not sampled","Summing token attributes over sampled spans without weighting","A gateway token metric with low-cardinality dimensions","Per-operation token histograms"],"answers":[0,2],"explain":"Counters and gateway metrics are unsampled aggregates. Sampled spans distort totals, and per-operation histograms are for percentiles and outliers, not total usage or cost."},{"id":"w15-t04-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A per-user token metric in API Management loses series without errors. Why?","options":["Per-user metrics are not supported by Application Insights","Streaming disables metrics","The policy allows 100 unique values per dimension and drops the excess silently","The model deployment has no usage data"],"answer":2,"explain":"API Management enforces 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active time series per namespace and drops the excess silently. The other options do not explain vanishing series."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which metrics hold token totals, and which are for percentiles?","back":"Counters `gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` hold totals. Per-operation histograms are for percentiles and outliers, not totals or cost."},{"front":"Are cache-read and reasoning tokens extra on top of input and output?","back":"No. Cache reads are a subset of input tokens and reasoning tokens are a subset of output tokens."},{"front":"What are the API Management token metric limits?","back":"Up to 5 custom dimensions, 100 unique values per dimension and 1,000 active series per namespace. Excess is dropped silently."}],"related":["w15-t02","w15-t07","w15-t10","w16-t01","w16-t07"],"refs":["r2","r5","r10","r16","r15"]},{"id":"w15-t05","order":5,"group":"Distributed tracing","title":"Trace propagation across services","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"One user request crosses agents, MCP servers, A2A peers and queues. Propagating W3C trace context over each hop, in HTTP headers or MCP params._meta, keeps it one trace. Some hops need correlation ids instead.","useIf":"A single request touches several processes and you want one trace, not five unrelated ones.","avoidIf":"You forward baggage or trace context to parties that should not see it, or assume every framework propagates it for you.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"W3C Trace Context defines the `traceparent` header: `00-<32 hex trace id>-<16 hex parent id>-<2 hex flags>`, plus `tracestate`. Level 1 is a W3C Recommendation (2021-11-23). Level 2 is still a Candidate Recommendation Draft (2024-03-28) [[r8]]. Over HTTP, OpenTelemetry instrumentation injects and extracts these headers for you."},{"t":"p","md":"**MCP** has no HTTP-only assumption, so the conventions inject `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage` into the request's `params._meta` using unprefixed keys (SEP-414). The server extracts them as the remote parent, and the MCP client span becomes the parent of the server span whatever the transport. The MCP revision 2026-07-28 reserves these three keys in `_meta` as an exception to the vendor-prefix rule and requires W3C formats [[r6]] [[r7]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w15-t05-d1","title":"Context crossing an A2A hop and an MCP hop","alt":"An orchestrator sends an A2A request with a traceparent HTTP header to a remote agent. The remote agent calls an MCP server, putting traceparent into params underscore meta. All spans share one trace id.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant O as Orchestrator\n  participant R as Remote agent\n  participant M as MCP server\n  O->>R: A2A request, traceparent header\n  R->>M: tools/call, params._meta traceparent\n  M-->>R: Result\n  R-->>O: Task with artifact"},{"t":"p","md":"**A2A** defines no tracing fields of its own. Its enterprise guidance says clients and servers should participate in distributed tracing, for example by propagating W3C Trace Context headers over HTTP, and should log `taskId`, `sessionId`, correlation ids and trace context [[r9]]. The Agent Framework documentation does not say whether its A2A client and server forward `traceparent`: UNVERIFIED (checked 2026-10-13)."},{"t":"table","caption":"How context crosses each hop","head":["Hop","Carrier","What to check"],"rows":[["Service to service over HTTP","`traceparent` and `tracestate` headers","Both sides use W3C propagators and make consistent sampling decisions"],["Agent to MCP server","`params._meta` keys `traceparent`, `tracestate`, `baggage`","Agent Framework Python injects them only for MCP sessions it opens itself, not for provider-hosted MCP tools; .NET behavior is UNVERIFIED [[r10]]"],["Agent to A2A peer","HTTP headers by guidance, no spec field","Test it, and log `taskId` and `contextId` as a fallback"],["Async hop (queue, webhook, background task)","Store the context with the message or task id","Link the continuation to the original trace (practice, not a spec rule)"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"Baggage travels to third parties","md":"Baggage is propagated across service boundaries and to third-party APIs, so do not put passwords, keys or personal data in it [[r17]]. Strip or restrict baggage at organization boundaries."}],"whereToUse":["A multi-agent system where an orchestrator calls specialist agents over A2A","An agent that calls MCP servers run by other teams and needs end-to-end latency","A background or durable run that continues after the original request returned","Debugging which hop added latency or lost an error","Security reviews of what identifiers leave the organization with each request"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Propagate W3C trace context on every hop you control, including MCP `_meta`","Use client-opened MCP transports when you need an end-to-end trace","Log `taskId`, `contextId` and trace id together at every A2A boundary"],"avoid":["Do not assume provider-hosted MCP tools or third-party agents continue your trace","Do not forward baggage with sensitive values to external parties","Do not let each service sample independently: a trace must be kept or dropped as a whole"],"flips":["A hop is a provider-hosted tool: accept a gap and correlate by call ids and timestamps","The peer is another organization: propagate trace context only if both sides agree, and keep correlation ids either way","The work continues after a restart: persist context with the task and link the new trace to the old one"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to correlate work across hops","head":["Mechanism","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["W3C trace context in headers","HTTP hops between instrumented services","Hops that are not HTTP or are not instrumented","Needs consistent propagators and sampling"],["MCP `params._meta` context","Agent to MCP calls, any transport","Provider-hosted MCP tools","Only works if client and server both honor it [[r6]]"],["Correlation ids (`taskId`, call ids)","Black-box peers and async hops","You need timing across hops","Manual joins, no waterfall view"],["Span links","Continuations after queues or restarts","Simple synchronous calls","More work to build and view"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A trace stops at the agent's `execute_tool` span and the MCP server starts a new trace","cause":"The tool was a provider-hosted MCP tool, so the framework did not inject `params._meta` context","fix":"Use a client-opened MCP transport (for example `MCPStreamableHTTPTool`) when you need end-to-end tracing [[r10]]"},{"symptom":"The peer agent's work appears as a separate trace","cause":"No `traceparent` header was sent or the peer ignored it, and the A2A spec has no tracing fields","fix":"Send the header, verify the peer extracts it and log `taskId` on both sides as a fallback [[r9]]"},{"symptom":"Only part of each trace is stored","cause":"Downstream services made independent sampling decisions instead of honoring the parent's","fix":"Use consistent trace-ID-based head sampling, or tail sampling in a collector, so a trace is kept or dropped as a whole [[r20]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t05-s1","title":"An external partner's agent in the middle of a request","situation":"An orchestrator calls a partner's agent over A2A. The partner runs its own telemetry. Requests contain customer data and legal limits what identifiers cross the boundary.","constraints":["Other organization","No shared observability backend","Baggage may carry internal labels"],"unknowns":["Does the partner extract `traceparent`?","Does a contract cover sharing trace ids?"],"question":"How do you keep the request debuggable?","options":[{"name":"Send `traceparent` only if agreed, strip baggage, and log `taskId` and `contextId` on both sides","verdict":"best","why":"You get a joinable trail without leaking internal labels or assuming the partner continues your trace.","tradeoff":"No single waterfall: joins are manual."},{"name":"Forward all context including baggage","verdict":"poor","why":"Baggage travels to third parties and could expose internal tags or user data.","tradeoff":"Right inside your own trust boundary."},{"name":"Send nothing and rely on timestamps","verdict":"good","why":"Zero leakage.","tradeoff":"Hard to join incidents and impossible to attribute latency by hop."}],"flips":[{"if":"Both companies run compatible tracing and agree to share ids","then":"Propagate `traceparent` and link traces."},{"if":"The partner cannot join on `taskId`","then":"Ask for a correlation header in the contract."}],"takeaway":"Cross-organization context sharing is a decision, not a default."},{"id":"w15-t05-s2","title":"An agent using a provider-hosted MCP tool","situation":"An agent uses an MCP tool hosted by the model provider's runtime. Slow tool calls are the main incident cause and the team wants end-to-end traces.","constraints":["Provider makes the MCP call","End-to-end tracing wanted","Tool server is run by your own team"],"unknowns":["Can the agent open the MCP connection itself?","Does the tool server extract `_meta` context?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Switch to a client-opened MCP transport so the framework injects `params._meta`","verdict":"best","why":"The agent process makes the call, so context is injected and the server span joins the trace.","tradeoff":"The agent needs network access to the server and you lose provider-side convenience."},{"name":"Keep the hosted tool and correlate by call id and timestamps","verdict":"good","why":"No code change and still lets you join logs.","tradeoff":"No waterfall and slower diagnosis."},{"name":"Assume propagation works and skip verification","verdict":"poor","why":"The framework does not inject context for provider-managed MCP calls, so traces break silently.","tradeoff":"Safe only if the tool server already links by other means."}],"flips":[{"if":"The tool is third-party hosted","then":"Keep the hosted tool and rely on correlation ids."},{"if":"The framework is .NET","then":"Verify behavior directly, since the docs describe it for Python only."}],"takeaway":"Check who makes the call, because that process injects the context."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"How does trace context travel on an MCP `tools/call` request?","options":["In `params._meta` with the keys `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage`","In a custom header named `X-MCP-Trace`","In the tool's input arguments","It cannot travel on MCP"],"answer":0,"explain":"The conventions inject W3C context into `params._meta`, and MCP revision 2026-07-28 reserves those unprefixed keys. Tool arguments are model-controlled data and should not carry trace context."},{"id":"w15-t05-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"A trace breaks at an MCP call. Which causes fit the documented Agent Framework behavior? Select all that apply.","options":["The tool is a provider-managed MCP tool, so the provider runtime makes the call","The agent opened the MCP session itself with a streamable HTTP tool","The server does not extract `_meta` trace context","A global propagator was configured to something other than W3C"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Hosted tools skip injection, a server that ignores `_meta` cannot join the trace, and the framework injects through the global propagators, so a mismatched propagator changes the format. A client-opened session is the case that does inject context."},{"id":"w15-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"What is the main risk of forwarding W3C `baggage` on every call?","options":["It disables sampling","It doubles token usage","It breaks `traceparent`","It propagates across service boundaries and to third-party APIs, so sensitive values can leak"],"answer":3,"explain":"Baggage is propagated to downstream and third-party services. It does not affect sampling, tokens or `traceparent`."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does a `traceparent` header look like?","back":"`00-<32 hex trace id>-<16 hex parent id>-<2 hex flags>`. W3C Trace Context Level 1 is a Recommendation (2021-11-23)."},{"front":"Where does MCP carry trace context?","back":"In `params._meta` as `traceparent`, `tracestate` and `baggage`. MCP 2026-07-28 reserves these keys."},{"front":"When does Agent Framework not inject MCP trace context?","back":"For provider-hosted or provider-managed MCP tools, because the provider runtime makes the call. Client-opened MCP sessions do get context in Python."},{"front":"Does A2A define tracing fields?","back":"No. Its guidance says to propagate W3C Trace Context over HTTP and to log `taskId`, `sessionId` and correlation ids."}],"related":["w15-t03","w15-t07","w09-t03","w09-t09","w08-t09","w08-t10"],"refs":["r6","r7","r8","r9","r10","r17","r20"]},{"id":"w15-t06","order":6,"group":"Privacy and volume","title":"Content capture, redaction and privacy","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Prompts, outputs and tool payloads are the best debugging evidence and the riskiest telemetry. The GenAI conventions make content capture opt-in, and production setups usually keep it off spans, in a governed store, referenced from the trace.","useIf":"You decide whether and how telemetry may hold prompts, retrieved text and tool results.","avoidIf":"You flip one global content flag in production without redaction, retention and access rules.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The conventions say instrumentations SHOULD NOT capture content by default. The opt-in attributes are `gen_ai.system_instructions`, `gen_ai.input.messages`, `gen_ai.output.messages`, `gen_ai.tool.definitions`, `gen_ai.tool.call.arguments` and `gen_ai.tool.call.result`. They describe three patterns: (1) record nothing, the default; (2) record content on spans, suited to pre-production or telemetry that meets your privacy rules; (3) store content externally and put **references** on spans, recommended for production. An in-process upload hook runs regardless of the span sampling decision and independent of the opt-in flags, and the spec still lists TODO for a common reference format [[r4]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Agent Framework's switch is `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` (default false) in Python and `EnableSensitiveData` in .NET. Sensitive data means prompts, responses, tool arguments and tool results. In workflows it also controls `executor.input`, `executor.output` and `message.content` [[r10]] [[r11]]. Foundry and the Azure SDKs use `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`, and the older classic page used `AZURE_TRACING_GEN_AI_CONTENT_RECORDING_ENABLED`. The `@trace_function` decorator always records parameters and return values regardless of that flag [[r13]]."},{"t":"dials","id":"w15-t06-policy","title":"Choose a content capture policy","prompt":"Flip the constraints. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"sens","label":"Data sensitivity","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Synthetic or public"},{"id":"pii","label":"Personal data"},{"id":"reg","label":"Regulated or confidential"}]},{"id":"need","label":"Debugging need","values":[{"id":"meta","label":"Run shape is enough"},{"id":"text","label":"Must read the text"}]}],"options":[{"id":"none","name":"Metadata only","summary":"Default: no content in telemetry."},{"id":"spans","name":"Content on spans","summary":"Prompts and outputs as span attributes."},{"id":"ext","name":"External store with references","summary":"Content in a governed store, ids on spans."},{"id":"redact","name":"Redacted content on spans","summary":"Scrub patterns before export."}],"scores":{"none":{"sens":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Safe but gives up easy evidence."},"pii":{"s":1,"why":"Avoids storing personal data."},"reg":{"s":2,"why":"Strongest default for regulated data."}},"need":{"meta":{"s":2,"why":"Shape answers the question at no risk."},"text":{"s":-2,"why":"Cannot reproduce a wrong answer."}}},"spans":{"sens":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"No privacy cost and the simplest debugging."},"pii":{"s":-1,"why":"Personal data lands in the general telemetry store."},"reg":{"s":-2,"why":"Telemetry rarely meets regulated-data controls."}},"need":{"meta":{"s":-1,"why":"Adds volume and risk for no benefit."},"text":{"s":2,"why":"Content sits next to timing in one trace."}}},"ext":{"sens":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Works but is more machinery than needed."},"pii":{"s":2,"why":"Separate access, retention and deletion."},"reg":{"s":2,"why":"Content lives under data controls, traces carry only ids."}},"need":{"meta":{"s":-1,"why":"Overbuilt if shape is enough."},"text":{"s":2,"why":"Content is retrievable by authorized people."}}},"redact":{"sens":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Harmless but unnecessary."},"pii":{"s":1,"why":"Helps, but pattern scrubbing misses cases."},"reg":{"s":0,"why":"Redaction is not a guarantee for regulated data."}},"need":{"meta":{"s":-1,"why":"Extra processing with no need."},"text":{"s":1,"why":"Readable text with known identifiers removed."}}}},"takeaway":"Default to shape only. Move content to a governed store when you must read it, and capture on spans only for data you may freely keep."}],"whereToUse":["Pre-production debugging with synthetic prompts and full content on spans","Production assistants over personal or confidential data, where content is stored by reference","Evaluation pipelines that score sampled conversations and need the text under controls","Incident review where a restricted group may open a specific conversation","Policy documents that state what telemetry may hold and for how long"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Keep content off by default and enable it per environment and per purpose","Store production content externally with references on spans, short retention and role-based access","Redact known identifiers before anything leaves the process, and test the redaction"],"avoid":["Do not enable content capture globally in production because it was needed once","Do not forget that decorators such as `@trace_function` record arguments regardless of the flag","Do not let operations staff and data owners share one access role"],"flips":["Regulation forbids storing content: keep shape only and reproduce issues with synthetic inputs","Support must reproduce wrong answers: add a break-glass path that retrieves the stored conversation for one ticket","You use a hosted backend you do not control: assume content on spans is visible to the vendor and decide accordingly"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Content capture patterns","head":["Pattern","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Record nothing (default)","Regulated data, most production traffic","You must read the text to debug","Hard to reproduce quality issues"],["Content on spans","Development and synthetic data","Personal or confidential data","Attribute size limits and wide access to content"],["External store with references","Production with controls","A tiny team with no storage owner","Another system to secure, retain and delete from [[r4]]"],["Redacted content on spans","Moderate sensitivity with a tested scrubber","Regulated data","Imperfect redaction and processing cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Prompts show up in Application Insights although sensitive data is off","cause":"A `@trace_function` decorated function records its parameters and return values regardless of the flag","fix":"Remove the decorator from sensitive paths or redact arguments before the call [[r13]]"},{"symptom":"Prompts and outputs appear twice on parent and child spans in .NET","cause":"Both the chat client and the agent were instrumented with sensitive data on","fix":"Instrument one layer only [[r10]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t06-s1","title":"A pre-production agent with synthetic data","situation":"A team builds an agent and tests it with synthetic prompts. They want to read full prompts and tool results while tuning behavior. Telemetry goes to a development backend.","constraints":["Synthetic data only","Development backend","Debugging need is high"],"unknowns":["Could real data reach this environment later?","Who can read the dev backend?"],"question":"What content policy do you set?","options":[{"name":"Content on spans, enabled only in the development environment","verdict":"best","why":"No privacy cost and the fastest debugging.","tradeoff":"The setting must not be copied to production."},{"name":"Metadata only","verdict":"good","why":"Safe everywhere.","tradeoff":"Slows tuning because you cannot read prompts."},{"name":"External store with references","verdict":"poor","why":"It adds a storage system and access flow with no data sensitivity to justify it.","tradeoff":"Right once real user data appears."}],"flips":[{"if":"Real user prompts reach the environment","then":"Move to external storage or metadata only."},{"if":"The dev backend is shared widely","then":"Restrict who can read content attributes."}],"takeaway":"Capture freely only where the data is yours to share."},{"id":"w15-t06-s2","title":"Production over regulated records with a support team","situation":"A production assistant handles confidential case records. Support needs to reproduce a wrong answer for a specific ticket. Auditors require retention limits and access logs.","constraints":["Regulated, confidential data","Per-ticket reproduction needed","Retention and access logging required"],"unknowns":["Who may open a stored conversation?","How long must content be retained?"],"question":"How do you capture content?","options":[{"name":"External content store with references on spans, short retention, role-based access and audit logs","verdict":"best","why":"Traces carry ids only, and authorized people retrieve content for one ticket under logged access.","tradeoff":"You build and run another governed store."},{"name":"Content on spans in the shared telemetry backend","verdict":"poor","why":"Many engineers can read it and retention follows the telemetry store, not the data rules.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for non-sensitive data."},{"name":"Metadata only and ask users to resend inputs","verdict":"good","why":"No stored content.","tradeoff":"Slow, unreliable reproduction and poor user experience."}],"flips":[{"if":"No content may be stored at all","then":"Use metadata only and synthetic reproduction."},{"if":"Only a few conversations per month need review","then":"Capture content only for flagged or failed runs."}],"takeaway":"Separate who sees run shape from who sees user content."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t06-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Which pattern do the GenAI conventions recommend for production?","options":["Record content on every span","Store content externally and put references on spans","Record content only on the root span","Never record anything, including references"],"answer":1,"explain":"The conventions list three patterns and recommend external storage with references for production. Content on spans suits pre-production or telemetry that meets privacy rules."},{"id":"w15-t06-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each switch to the layer it controls.","pairs":[{"l":"`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA`","r":"Agent Framework telemetry content"},{"l":"`OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT`","r":"OTel and Azure SDK message content"},{"l":"`AZURE_TRACING_GEN_AI_CONTENT_RECORDING_ENABLED`","r":"Older classic Foundry content recording"},{"l":"`@trace_function`","r":"Always records parameters and return values"}],"explain":"Three similar flags exist for three layers, and the decorator records arguments regardless of any of them."},{"id":"w15-t06-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Sensitive data is off in Agent Framework, but a tool argument containing a customer id appears in traces. What is the likeliest cause?","options":["The backend ignores the flag","Sampling is set too high","A `@trace_function` decorator on that function records parameters and returns regardless of the flag","The model logs its own prompts"],"answer":2,"explain":"The decorator always records parameters and return values. The backend cannot change what the process exports, sampling affects volume not content, and the model does not write your telemetry."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the default for content capture in the GenAI conventions?","back":"Off. Instrumentations SHOULD NOT capture content by default. The content attributes are opt-in."},{"front":"Which content pattern is recommended for production?","back":"Store content externally and record references on spans. Content on spans suits pre-production or data you may freely keep."},{"front":"What does `@trace_function` ignore?","back":"The sensitive-data flag. It always records parameters and return values."}],"related":["w15-t01","w15-t07","w15-t09","w07-t16","w17-t03"],"refs":["r4","r10","r11","r12","r13"]},{"id":"w15-t07","order":7,"group":"Privacy and volume","title":"Sampling and retention","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Agent traces are large, so most systems keep only a share. Head sampling decides at the start of a trace, tail sampling decides after seeing it, and metrics are never sampled. Retention tiers then set how long what you kept stays searchable.","useIf":"Trace volume or cost is climbing and you must decide what to keep, for how long and where totals will come from.","avoidIf":"You compute token totals from sampled traces or sample so hard that the failing runs vanish.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Head sampling** decides as early as possible, without looking at the whole trace. The common form is consistent probability sampling by trace id, for example 5 percent, which keeps or drops whole traces. It is simple and cheap, but it cannot guarantee that traces containing errors are kept. **Tail sampling** decides after all or most spans of a trace are complete, so it can always keep errors, keep slow traces and apply different rates per service. It needs a stateful collector component, is harder to operate and tends to be vendor specific. Large systems often combine them: a coarse head sample protects the pipeline and tail sampling makes the fine decisions [[r20]]."},{"t":"p","md":"In Application Insights the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro for .NET, Node.js and Python defaults to **rate-limited sampling at five traces per second** for Azure Monitor export. You can choose a fixed ratio between 0 and 1 or a rate limit. Python reads `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER` and `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG`, not the older `sampling_ratio` and `traces_per_second`. If unsure, start with a fixed 5 percent (0.05). **Metrics are not sampled**, so alert on metrics. Ingestion sampling and a daily cap are last-resort cost guards. An App Service tutorial says the exporter applies adaptive sampling by default, but the sampling page is the more authoritative source [[r15]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where each signal stands","head":["Signal","Sampled?","Use it for"],"rows":[["Metrics (token counters, durations)","No","Totals, cost, SLIs and alerts"],["Traces with head sampling","Yes, whole traces by ratio or rate","Typical debugging and latency waterfalls"],["Traces with tail sampling","Yes, by rule after the trace ends","Keeping errors, slow runs and rare agents"],["Content in an external store","Your policy","Reading text for flagged or sampled runs"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"kql","title":"Check how much telemetry survives sampling (Microsoft Learn query)","code":"union requests,dependencies,pageViews,browserTimings,exceptions,traces\n| where timestamp > ago(1d)\n| summarize RetainedPercentage = 100/avg(itemCount) by bin(timestamp, 1h), itemType","caption":"Weight sums by `itemCount` when you total token attributes from retained spans."},{"t":"p","md":"Retention is a separate dial. The Foundry portal Traces tab shows 90 days, while backend retention follows your Application Insights settings [[r12]]. Keep long retention for metrics and aggregated views, short retention for detailed traces, and the shortest for any stored content (see content capture, redaction and privacy)."}],"whereToUse":["Cost control for an agent platform that emits many spans per run","Making sure every failed or slow agent run is kept while healthy runs are thinned","Setting export rates for an Application Insights deployment","Explaining why a load test shows fewer traces than requests","Retention policy design for traces, metrics and stored content"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Head sampling at a fixed ratio when volume is high and failures are not rare","Tail sampling in a collector when you must keep all errors and slow runs","Unsampled metrics for totals, SLIs and alerts, plus sampled traces for explanation"],"avoid":["Do not sample at all if you produce tens of small traces per second or are legally barred from dropping data","Do not total tokens or cost from sampled spans without weighting by `itemCount`","Do not use ingestion sampling or a daily cap as your main strategy"],"flips":["Failures are rare and you must keep every one: switch from head to tail sampling","A load test looks incomplete: raise the rate limit or use a fixed ratio for the test, since bursts above five traces per second are dropped by default","Volume is small: keep everything and skip the operational cost of a sampler"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to control trace volume","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["No sampling","Tens of traces per second or fewer, or no-drop rules","Volume is large","Highest storage and ingestion cost"],["Head sampling (ratio or rate)","High volume, uniform traffic","You must keep every error","May drop the traces you wanted"],["Tail sampling (collector)","Keep errors, slow runs and rare agents","A small team without collector operations","Stateful, harder to run, often vendor specific [[r20]]"],["Ingestion sampling or daily cap","Emergency cost control","Normal operation","Drops data without regard to value"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A load test shows far fewer traces than requests","cause":"Rate-limited sampling at five traces per second drops bursts above that rate","fix":"Use a fixed ratio or a higher rate limit for the test and confirm with the retained-percentage query [[r15]]"},{"symptom":"The traces you wanted, the errors, are missing","cause":"Head sampling decided before the error happened","fix":"Add tail sampling that keeps error and slow traces [[r20]]"},{"symptom":"Token totals computed from spans are far too low","cause":"The sums ignore that spans are sampled","fix":"Use unsampled counters for totals, or weight span sums by `itemCount` [[r15]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t07-s1","title":"Two thousand traces per second and rare failures","situation":"An agent platform emits about 2,000 traces per second. Failures are well under 1 percent but each one matters. Storage cost is the main complaint.","constraints":["High volume","Rare but important failures","Cost pressure"],"unknowns":["Can the team run a collector?","Does the backend offer tail sampling?"],"question":"What sampling do you use?","options":[{"name":"Tail sampling that keeps all errors and slow runs plus a small baseline share, with unsampled metrics for totals","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps the traces that matter and cuts cost on the healthy majority.","tradeoff":"A stateful collector tier to run, monitor and scale."},{"name":"Fixed 5 percent head sampling","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap, simple and keeps whole traces.","tradeoff":"Drops about 95 percent of failures along with healthy traces."},{"name":"Keep everything and set a daily ingestion cap","verdict":"poor","why":"The cap drops data by time of day, not by value, and cost stays high until it triggers.","tradeoff":"Reasonable as an emergency guard on top of a real strategy."}],"flips":[{"if":"No team can operate a collector","then":"Use head sampling and alert on error metrics."},{"if":"Volume falls below tens of traces per second","then":"Keep everything."}],"takeaway":"Keep by value when failures are rare."},{"id":"w15-t07-s2","title":"A regulated workflow that may not drop traces","situation":"An internal approval workflow produces 30 small traces per second. Auditors require every run to be reconstructable for a year, but content must not be stored in traces.","constraints":["Low volume","No-drop requirement","Content excluded from traces"],"unknowns":["Can unsampled data go to cheap storage?","What detail must a reconstruction contain?"],"question":"How do you handle volume and retention?","options":[{"name":"No sampling, shape-only spans, tiered retention (short hot, long cold storage)","verdict":"best","why":"At this volume sampling saves little and the audit needs every run.","tradeoff":"Higher storage cost and a tiering design."},{"name":"5 percent head sampling","verdict":"poor","why":"It drops 95 percent of runs, which violates the audit requirement.","tradeoff":"Right for volumes where dropping is allowed."},{"name":"Tail sampling that keeps errors only","verdict":"good","why":"Cheaper and keeps failures.","tradeoff":"Healthy runs are not reconstructable."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume grows to thousands per second","then":"Route unsampled data to low-cost storage and keep sampled data hot."},{"if":"The no-drop rule is lifted","then":"Move to tail sampling."}],"takeaway":"When you may not drop data, spend on retention tiers instead of sampling."},{"id":"w15-t07-s3","title":"A load test with missing traces","situation":"A load test sends 100 requests per second for ten minutes. The backend shows far fewer traces than requests, and the team suspects data loss in the app.","constraints":["Default distro settings","Burst load","Need a faithful test"],"unknowns":["What sampler and rate is configured?","Are metrics complete?"],"question":"What do you check and change?","options":[{"name":"Check the sampler settings and retained percentage, then use a fixed ratio or higher rate for the test","verdict":"best","why":"The default rate-limited sampler caps at five traces per second, which explains the gap.","tradeoff":"Higher volume and cost during the test."},{"name":"Increase application replicas","verdict":"poor","why":"The limit is the sampler, not capacity.","tradeoff":"Right if requests themselves are failing."},{"name":"Trust the traces and report data loss","verdict":"poor","why":"Missing sampled traces are expected, and the app is not dropping data.","tradeoff":"Use metrics to confirm request counts."}],"flips":[{"if":"Metrics also show fewer requests","then":"The app or network is dropping load, so investigate that."},{"if":"Production volume resembles the test","then":"Choose the ratio deliberately and document it."}],"takeaway":"Know your sampler before you trust a trace count."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which sampling approach can guarantee that every trace containing an error is kept?","options":["Fixed-ratio head sampling","Tail sampling that inspects the completed trace","Rate-limited head sampling","A daily ingestion cap"],"answer":1,"explain":"Head sampling decides before the trace content is known, so it cannot ensure errors are kept. Tail sampling decides after the trace completes and can keep errors and slow runs. A daily cap drops data without regard to value."},{"id":"w15-t07-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A sampled span stream shows RetainedPercentage of 10 percent. A retained span reports 1,000 input tokens. Roughly how many tokens does that span represent when weighted by `itemCount`?","answer":10000,"tolerancePct":0,"unit":"tokens","hint":"RetainedPercentage = 100 / average itemCount.","explain":"A 10 percent retention means itemCount is about 10, so each retained span stands for about 10 spans: 1,000 x 10 = 10,000 tokens."},{"id":"w15-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements about sampling in Application Insights are correct? Select all that apply.","options":["The Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro defaults to rate-limited sampling at five traces per second","Metrics are not sampled, so alerts should use metrics","Python configures sampling with `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER` and `OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG`","Traces are never sampled by default"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The distro defaults to five traces per second, metrics are not sampled and Python uses the standard OTel environment variables. The fourth statement contradicts the default."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the difference between head and tail sampling?","back":"Head decides at the start of a trace and cannot see errors. Tail decides after the trace completes, so it can keep errors and slow runs, but needs a stateful collector."},{"front":"What does the Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro sample by default?","back":"Rate-limited sampling at five traces per second for Azure Monitor export. Bursts above that are dropped."},{"front":"Why alert on metrics instead of traces?","back":"Metrics are not sampled, so counts and rates are accurate. Use traces to explain an alert."}],"related":["w15-t04","w15-t05","w15-t06","w15-t08","w16-t01"],"refs":["r12","r15","r20"]},{"id":"w15-t08","order":8,"group":"Platforms","title":"Foundry tracing and Application Insights","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Foundry can collect traces for its agents server-side after you connect an Application Insights resource to the project, with no code change. Client-side tracing and the Agents view add depth, and KQL over the dependencies table answers custom questions.","useIf":"Your agents run in Foundry Agent Service, or you want one Application Insights resource to hold traces from Foundry and your own code.","avoidIf":"You assume server-side tracing covers every agent type, or that Foundry traces and Agent Framework telemetry are the same pipeline.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Server-side tracing** turns on after you connect an Application Insights resource to the Foundry project. No code change is needed. It is generally available for prompt and hosted agents and in preview for workflow and external agents. Foundry stores traces in Application Insights using the OTel GenAI conventions, and the portal Traces tab shows 90 days. You need the Log Analytics Reader role, plus Privileged Monitoring Data Reader if the tables are protected [[r12]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Client-side tracing** (preview) instruments your own code that talks to Foundry. Set `AZURE_EXPERIMENTAL_ENABLE_GENAI_TRACING=true` (Python) or `AppContext.SetSwitch(\"Azure.Experimental.EnableGenAITracing\", true)` (.NET) before creating the client. Traces typically appear within a few minutes (2 to 5 minutes on the client-side page). Message content recording uses `OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=true`, and `@trace_function` records parameters and return values regardless [[r12]] [[r13]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Foundry server-side versus Agent Framework client-side telemetry [[r12]] [[r10]]","head":["Question","Foundry server-side tracing","Agent Framework or SDK telemetry"],"rows":[["Who produces spans?","Foundry, for agents it hosts","Your process, through OpenTelemetry"],["Setup","Connect Application Insights to the project","Enable instrumentation, configure an exporter"],["Covers","Prompt and hosted agents (GA), workflow and external agents (preview)","Any code you run, including external agents"],["Content control","Content recording flag","`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` or `EnableSensitiveData`"],["Backend","Application Insights","Any OTLP backend, including the same Application Insights"]]},{"t":"p","md":"In Application Insights the **Agents (Preview)** blade is built on the OTel GenAI semantics. It shows agent runs, tool calls, models and GenAI errors, can sort traces by tokens used, and offers prebuilt Grafana dashboards for Agent Framework, Agent Framework workflows, Foundry and coding agents. It relies on `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` and `gen_ai.usage.output_tokens`. Spans land in the `dependencies` table with attributes in `customDimensions` [[r14]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"kql","title":"Confirm gen_ai attributes reach Application Insights (Microsoft Learn query)","code":"dependencies\n| where timestamp > ago(1h)\n| where customDimensions has \"gen_ai.agent.name\"\n| project timestamp, name, customDimensions\n| take 20"},{"t":"code","lang":"kql","title":"Tokens per agent (composed from documented attribute names, verify against your data)","code":"dependencies\n| where timestamp > ago(1d)\n| where customDimensions has \"gen_ai.usage.input_tokens\"\n| extend agent = tostring(customDimensions[\"gen_ai.agent.name\"]),\n         inTok = toint(customDimensions[\"gen_ai.usage.input_tokens\"]),\n         outTok = toint(customDimensions[\"gen_ai.usage.output_tokens\"])\n| summarize calls = sum(itemCount), inputTokens = sum(inTok * itemCount), outputTokens = sum(outTok * itemCount) by agent\n| order by outputTokens desc"}],"whereToUse":["Prompt and hosted agents in Foundry where you want traces without code changes","A single Application Insights resource that holds Foundry traces and your own services' telemetry","Investigating tool calls and model errors in the Agents view","KQL reports of tokens, runs and p95 durations per agent","Grafana dashboards for agent fleets"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Connect Application Insights to the Foundry project for prompt and hosted agents","Add client-side tracing for your own orchestration code and external agents","Use KQL on `dependencies` and `customDimensions` for questions the blades do not answer"],"avoid":["Do not rely on preview tracing paths (workflow, external and client-side) for production SLAs","Do not forget to weight KQL sums by `itemCount` when sampling is on","Do not enable content recording only because the portal offers it"],"flips":["You need a non-Azure backend: export OTLP from your own code and keep Foundry traces for Foundry-hosted agents","Regulated content: keep content recording off and use shape signals","Workflow or external agents are core: plan for preview behavior and re-check when GA labels change"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Where agent traces can live","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry portal plus Application Insights","Agents hosted in Foundry, no code change","Agents run elsewhere","Preview paths for workflow, external and client-side"],["Application Insights with Grafana","You want dashboards and KQL across services","You need LLM-specific eval and prompt tooling","Agents view and some dashboards are preview [[r14]]"],["Local Aspire Dashboard","Development on a laptop","Production","Not a production backend (ports 4317 and 18888 in the docs)"],["Third-party OTLP backend","You need its evals or prompt management","You want Foundry portal views","A second system and its data residency"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"No traces appear in the Foundry Traces tab","cause":"No Application Insights resource is connected to the project, or the reader lacks Log Analytics Reader","fix":"Connect the resource, grant Log Analytics Reader (and Privileged Monitoring Data Reader for protected tables) and wait a few minutes [[r12]]"},{"symptom":"Client-side spans never show up","cause":"The experimental tracing switch was set after the client was created, or not at all","fix":"Set `AZURE_EXPERIMENTAL_ENABLE_GENAI_TRACING` or the .NET switch before creating the client [[r13]]"},{"symptom":"Agents view panels are empty although traces exist","cause":"Spans lack `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` and `gen_ai.usage.output_tokens`, which the view relies on","fix":"Run the verification query and add or map the missing attributes [[r14]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t08-s1","title":"Prompt agents in Foundry, compliance wants traces, no code changes","situation":"A team runs several prompt agents in Foundry Agent Service. Compliance wants traces for investigations. The team has no appetite for instrumenting code.","constraints":["Prompt agents only","No code changes","Azure-centric"],"unknowns":["Is content recording allowed?","Who needs read access to the data?"],"question":"How do you get traces?","options":[{"name":"Connect Application Insights to the Foundry project and enable server-side tracing, content recording off","verdict":"best","why":"GA for prompt agents, no code change and the Traces tab and Agents view work out of the box.","tradeoff":"Tied to Application Insights, and content stays out of traces."},{"name":"Wrap every agent call in custom client tracing","verdict":"good","why":"Adds your own spans and context.","tradeoff":"Client-side tracing is preview and duplicates what the platform emits."},{"name":"Log requests and responses in an app database","verdict":"poor","why":"It stores content in a bespoke store without run structure.","tradeoff":"Right if auditors require conversation records as a separate system."}],"flips":[{"if":"Agents are workflow or external agents","then":"Expect preview behavior and add client-side tracing."},{"if":"Content must be readable for audits","then":"Use a governed content store, not trace attributes."}],"takeaway":"Platform-hosted agents get platform tracing for free."},{"id":"w15-t08-s2","title":"Foundry agents plus self-hosted orchestration code","situation":"An orchestrator runs on containers and calls both Foundry agents and a partner agent. The team wants one trace view and one place for KQL.","constraints":["Mixed hosting","One Application Insights wanted","Some preview features acceptable"],"unknowns":["Which hops propagate context?","Is the exporter configured in the orchestrator?"],"question":"What setup do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Server-side tracing for Foundry agents plus OpenTelemetry in the orchestrator exporting to the same Application Insights","verdict":"best","why":"Each part is traced by the layer that owns it and everything lands in one store.","tradeoff":"Two pipelines to configure and a preview client-side path."},{"name":"Only server-side tracing","verdict":"poor","why":"It misses your orchestrator and the partner hop.","tradeoff":"Enough when every agent is hosted in Foundry."},{"name":"Only client-side tracing","verdict":"good","why":"Covers your code and what you call.","tradeoff":"Misses server-side internals of Foundry agents."}],"flips":[{"if":"Foundry agents are only prompt agents","then":"Server-side tracing may be all you need."},{"if":"You need a vendor-neutral backend","then":"Export OTLP from the orchestrator and keep Foundry traces for Foundry agents."}],"takeaway":"Combine server-side and client-side tracing when hosting is mixed."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What turns on Foundry server-side tracing for prompt agents?","options":["Adding `UseOpenTelemetry` to every agent","Setting `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA=true`","Installing the Aspire Dashboard","Connecting an Application Insights resource to the Foundry project"],"answer":3,"explain":"Server-side tracing needs only the Application Insights connection and no code change. `UseOpenTelemetry` and `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` belong to Agent Framework telemetry, and Aspire is a local viewer."},{"id":"w15-t08-q2","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"Foundry traces are missing. Which checks are sensible? Select all that apply.","options":["An Application Insights resource is connected to the project","The reader holds Log Analytics Reader (and Privileged Monitoring Data Reader if tables are protected)","For client-side spans, the tracing switch was set before the client was created","The agent must be a Semantic Kernel plugin"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The connection, the reader roles and the client-side switch are the documented prerequisites. Semantic Kernel plugins are not a requirement."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which Foundry agent types have GA server-side tracing?","back":"Prompt and hosted agents. Workflow and external agents, and client-side tracing, are preview (checked 2026-10-13)."},{"front":"How long does the Foundry portal Traces tab show data?","back":"90 days. Backend retention follows your Application Insights settings."},{"front":"Where do GenAI spans land in Application Insights?","back":"In the `dependencies` table, with attributes in `customDimensions`."}],"related":["w15-t07","w15-t09","w15-t10","w12-t14","w16-t07"],"refs":["r10","r12","r13","r14"]},{"id":"w15-t09","order":9,"group":"Platforms","title":"Agent Framework built-in telemetry","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Agent Framework emits OpenTelemetry spans and metrics for agent runs, model calls and tool calls. You supply the exporter. Python instrumentation is on by default since python-1.6.0, .NET needs a wrapper, and sensitive content stays off unless you enable it.","useIf":"You build agents or workflows with Agent Framework and want traces and metrics in any OpenTelemetry backend.","avoidIf":"You assume telemetry flows by default: spans are created, but no exporter is installed automatically.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The framework follows the OTel GenAI conventions. Spans: `invoke_agent <agent_name>` at the top, `chat <model_name>` per model call and `execute_tool <function_name>` per tool. Metrics: `gen_ai.client.operation.duration` (histogram, seconds), `gen_ai.client.token.usage` (histogram, tokens) and `agent_framework.function.invocation.duration` (histogram, seconds). Workflows add `workflow.build`, `workflow.session` (.NET and Go) or `workflow.run` (Python), `workflow_invoke`, `executor.process {executor_id}`, `edge_group.process` and `message.send`. The docs' sample trace is dated 2025-09-25 and predates the current convention names, and which build emits which attribute and metric names is UNVERIFIED [[r10]] [[r11]]."},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Python","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"`configure_otel_providers()` reads the standard `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_*` variables, or set `ENABLE_CONSOLE_EXPORTERS=true`. You can also pass your own exporters, or configure your own SDK and call `enable_instrumentation(enable_sensitive_data=False)`. Instrumentation became on by default in python-1.6.0 (2026-05-21), but no exporter is installed automatically [[r10]] [[tr6]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"bash","title":"Environment variables from the docs (defaults in comments)","code":"ENABLE_INSTRUMENTATION=true          # default true\nENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA=false          # default false\nENABLE_CONSOLE_EXPORTERS=false       # default false\nOTEL_SERVICE_NAME=support-agent      # default agent_framework\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=grpc     # docs list grpc or http, default grpc\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317"}]},{"label":".NET","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Wrap the chat client or the agent with `.AsBuilder().UseOpenTelemetry(sourceName, cfg => cfg.EnableSensitiveData = ...)`. The default source name is `Experimental.Microsoft.Agents.AI`, and the tracer and meter providers must call `AddSource` with the same name. Instrumenting both layers duplicates prompts and responses when sensitive data is on, so pick one. The Harness wraps both layers by default (`OpenTelemetrySourceName`, `DisableOpenTelemetry`) [[r10]]."}]}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Sensitive data** means prompts, responses, tool arguments and tool results, and it is off by default. Turn instrumentation off with `ENABLE_INSTRUMENTATION=false`, `enable_instrumentation=False` on the agent or `disable_instrumentation()`. For local work, run the standalone Aspire Dashboard in Docker, set `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317` and open `http://localhost:18888` [[r10]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"MCP context only for sessions the agent opens","md":"In Python the framework injects W3C trace context into `params._meta` for `MCPStreamableHTTPTool`, `MCPStdioTool` and `MCPWebsocketTool` sessions it opens itself, not for hosted or provider-managed MCP tools. .NET behavior is UNVERIFIED because the docs describe it for Python only [[r10]]."}],"whereToUse":["Local development with the Aspire Dashboard to see agent, model and tool spans","Production export to Application Insights, Langfuse, LangSmith, Phoenix or any OTLP endpoint","Workflow debugging with executor and edge spans","Cost dashboards from the framework's duration and token metrics","Teams that must keep content off spans by default"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Configure an exporter explicitly, because the framework does not install one","Leave `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` false in production and capture content through a governed path","Instrument one layer in .NET, and let the Harness do both only when you use it"],"avoid":["Do not assume spans reach a backend because instrumentation is on by default","Do not point the default gRPC exporter at a backend that accepts only OTLP over HTTP","Do not build dashboards on metric names you have not seen in your backend"],"flips":["The backend accepts only HTTP OTLP (for example Langfuse): set the protocol to http","You run through Foundry hosted agents: use Foundry server-side tracing for the hosted part and framework telemetry for your own code","Telemetry overhead is a concern: disable it for hot paths or sample traces and keep metrics"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to get agent telemetry","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Framework built-in telemetry","Agent Framework apps, any OTLP backend","Agents run outside the framework","Names may differ from the newest conventions"],["Provider SDK instrumentation","Model-call spans for any code","You need agent and tool structure","No run-level nesting"],["Foundry server-side tracing","Agents hosted in Foundry","Self-hosted agents","Preview paths for some agent types [[r12]]"],["Hand-written spans","A niche step nobody instruments","Anything the framework already covers","Naming drift and maintenance"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Nothing arrives in the backend even though instrumentation is on","cause":"Spans are created by default, but no exporter is configured","fix":"Set `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_*`, enable console exporters for a check, or pass exporters in code [[r10]]"},{"symptom":"Langfuse shows nothing from a default Python agent","cause":"The framework's default OTLP protocol is gRPC and Langfuse accepts only OTLP over HTTP (HTTP/JSON or HTTP/protobuf)","fix":"Set the protocol to http and use the `/api/public/otel` endpoint with the required headers [[r17]]"},{"symptom":"Prompts are duplicated in .NET traces","cause":"Both the chat client and the agent were wrapped with sensitive data on","fix":"Instrument one layer only [[r10]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t09-s1","title":"A laptop workflow with a new agent","situation":"A developer builds an agent with Agent Framework and wants to see spans and token counts while iterating. Data is synthetic and the machine has Docker.","constraints":["Local development","Synthetic data","Fast feedback"],"unknowns":["Python or .NET?","Is the developer behind a proxy that blocks local ports?"],"question":"How do you view telemetry?","options":[{"name":"Run the Aspire Dashboard in Docker and point the OTLP endpoint at localhost:4317","verdict":"best","why":"It shows traces, logs and metrics locally with no cloud setup.","tradeoff":"Local only, so it does not replace a production backend."},{"name":"Console exporters","verdict":"good","why":"Zero infrastructure.","tradeoff":"Hard to read nested spans in a terminal."},{"name":"Export straight to the production backend with sensitive data on","verdict":"poor","why":"Development prompts mix into production telemetry and may expose test data.","tradeoff":"Fine only for a dedicated dev project in the backend."}],"flips":[{"if":"No Docker is available","then":"Use console exporters or a hosted dev project."},{"if":"The team wants shared traces","then":"Use a shared dev backend with restricted access."}],"takeaway":"Use a local viewer while iterating and keep content on only there."},{"id":"w15-t09-s2","title":"Sending Agent Framework traces to Langfuse","situation":"A team wants Agent Framework Python traces in Langfuse for prompt and eval tooling. The first attempt produced no data.","constraints":["Backend accepts OTLP over HTTP only","Framework default exporter is gRPC","Basic authentication required"],"unknowns":["Cloud region or self-hosted endpoint?","Where are the keys stored?"],"question":"What is the fix?","options":[{"name":"Set the OTLP protocol to http, use the Langfuse `/api/public/otel` endpoint and its auth header","verdict":"best","why":"It matches what Langfuse accepts: OTLP over HTTP, not gRPC.","tradeoff":"This follows from the two docs pages, not from a vendor statement, so verify with a test span."},{"name":"Run an OpenTelemetry Collector that receives gRPC and exports HTTP to Langfuse","verdict":"good","why":"Works with the default exporter and gives you tail sampling too.","tradeoff":"One more component to run."},{"name":"Keep gRPC and point it at Langfuse","verdict":"poor","why":"Langfuse does not support gRPC, so exports fail.","tradeoff":"Right for backends that accept gRPC OTLP."}],"flips":[{"if":"You already run a collector","then":"Send gRPC to it and let it export HTTP."},{"if":"Content must stay out of Langfuse","then":"Keep `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` false and check what the attributes hold."}],"takeaway":"Match the exporter protocol to the backend."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Python instrumentation is on by default, yet no data reaches the backend. Why?","options":["No exporter is installed automatically, so spans are created but not exported","Instrumentation turns off after one hour","The framework only exports to Application Insights","Sensitive data must be on to export anything"],"answer":0,"explain":"Spans are created but nothing is exported until you configure an exporter. The framework is backend-neutral and the sensitive-data flag only controls content."},{"id":"w15-t09-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each setting to its documented default.","pairs":[{"l":"`ENABLE_INSTRUMENTATION`","r":"true"},{"l":"`ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA`","r":"false"},{"l":"`OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`","r":"agent_framework"},{"l":"`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL`","r":"grpc"}],"explain":"These are the Python defaults in the docs checked 2026-10-13. Instrumentation became default-on in python-1.6.0, and content is off by default."},{"id":"w15-t09-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"In .NET you wrap both the chat client and the agent with `UseOpenTelemetry` and sensitive data is on. What happens?","options":["Traces are sampled twice as heavily","The second wrapper is ignored","Metrics are dropped","Prompts and responses appear on both spans, duplicating content"],"answer":3,"explain":"Instrumenting both layers duplicates content on both spans when sensitive data is on, so pick one layer. The Harness wraps both layers by default."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which spans does Agent Framework emit?","back":"`invoke_agent <agent_name>`, `chat <model_name>` and `execute_tool <function_name>`, plus workflow, executor and edge spans."},{"front":"What changed in python-1.6.0 (2026-05-21) for telemetry?","back":"Instrumentation became on by default. Spans are created, but no exporter is installed and sensitive data stays off."},{"front":"Why can a default Python agent send nothing to Langfuse?","back":"The default OTLP protocol is gRPC and Langfuse accepts only OTLP over HTTP. Set the protocol to http."}],"related":["w15-t03","w15-t06","w15-t08","w15-t12","w11-t26"],"refs":["r10","r11","r12","r17","tr6"]},{"id":"w15-t10","order":10,"group":"Operating","title":"Dashboards, SLIs, SLOs and alerts","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Turn telemetry into targets: pick a few SLIs that reflect what users feel (availability, time to first output, tool reliability, runaway runs, cost, quality), set SLOs from your own baseline and page on error-budget burn rate instead of raw thresholds.","useIf":"You must define what healthy means for an LLM or agent service, and when to wake someone up.","avoidIf":"You copy someone else's numbers or alert on every dip in a noisy quality score.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"An **SLI** is a measured ratio of good events to all events. An **SLO** is the target for it over a window. Google's SRE Workbook gives the method: choose SLIs from user experience, set the SLO from your own baseline and use the **error budget** (1 minus the SLO) to decide when to alert [[r21]]. The suggestions below are design ideas, not vendor numbers."},{"t":"table","caption":"SLIs for an agent run (design suggestions)","head":["SLI","How to measure","Signal"],"rows":[["Availability of runs","Share of `invoke_agent` spans without `error.type`","`gen_ai.invoke_agent.duration` with `error.type`"],["End-to-end latency","p95 run duration, excluding time paused for human approval","`gen_ai.invoke_agent.duration`"],["Time to first output","p95 first-chunk time for streaming","`gen_ai.client.inference.time_to_first_chunk`"],["Tool reliability","Error ratio and p95 duration per tool","`gen_ai.execute_tool.duration`"],["Runaway detection","p99 of model calls and tool calls per run","`gen_ai.invoke_agent.inference_calls`, `.tool_calls`"],["Cost","Tokens per successful task, cached share","`gen_ai.client.inference.usage.*` counters"],["Quality","Online evaluation pass rate on sampled traffic","Evaluation results (see online quality monitoring)"]]},{"t":"formula","title":"Burn rate","expr":"burn rate = observed error rate / (1 - SLO)","vars":[{"k":"SLO","v":"Target ratio of good events, for example 0.999 over 30 days"},{"k":"Error budget","v":"1 - SLO, for example 0.1 percent of events"}],"example":"With a 99.9 percent SLO over 30 days, a constant 0.1 percent error rate is a burn rate of 1 and spends exactly the budget. An error rate of 1.44 percent sustained for one hour is a burn rate of 14.4 and spends 2 percent of the 30-day budget (14.4 x 1 h / 720 h)."},{"t":"table","caption":"Starting alert policy from the SRE Workbook [[tr1]]","head":["Window","Budget consumed","Burn rate","Action"],"rows":[["1 hour","2 percent","14.4","Page"],["6 hours","5 percent","6","Page"],["3 days","10 percent","1","Ticket"]]},{"t":"p","md":"The Workbook recommends 2 percent in one hour and 5 percent in six hours as reasonable starting numbers for paging, and 10 percent in three days for tickets, and says the right values depend on the service. Use a short window of one twelfth of the long window to cut false alerts and speed recovery, and suppress duplicate pages when several rules fire together [[tr1]]. Alert on **metrics**, which are not sampled, and use traces to explain the alert [[r15]]."}],"whereToUse":["A customer-facing assistant with availability, first-token latency and quality targets","An agent platform where tool reliability and runaway runs drive incidents","A batch agent pipeline with a deadline-based completion SLI","Weekly reviews of error-budget spend and cost per successful task","On-call design: which signals page and which open tickets"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Define SLIs from user-visible outcomes and measure them from unsampled metrics","Use multi-window burn-rate alerts for paging and a slow-burn ticket alert","Exclude human approval waits from latency SLIs so slow people do not burn budget"],"avoid":["Do not page on raw token counts or single slow requests","Do not set SLOs without a measured baseline","Do not treat an LLM-judge score from a tiny sample as a paging signal"],"flips":["Traffic is low: widen windows or use ticket-level alerts, since a few failures can swing ratios","Quality is the product: promote a validated quality SLI, but keep it ticket-level until agreement with human review is established","Runs wait on humans: measure compute time and approval time as separate SLIs"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Alerting approaches","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Burn-rate alerts on SLOs","You have a clear SLO and enough traffic","Traffic is tiny or SLIs are undefined","More windows and thresholds to manage [[tr1]]"],["Static thresholds (for example p95 over 5 s)","Quick start, simple systems","Load or mix changes often","Noisy or late alerts"],["Anomaly detection on metrics","No stable baseline yet","You need explainable pages","Opaque behavior and tuning"],["Quality-score alerts from online evals","Silent failures are the main risk","The judge is untested or the sample is small","Cost and false alarms from noisy judges"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Pages fire constantly during busy hours and nobody trusts them","cause":"Static latency thresholds ignore load and prompt mix","fix":"Move to SLO burn-rate alerts with a short and a long window [[tr1]]"},{"symptom":"Latency SLO burns every time a human approval is pending","cause":"Approval waits are counted as service latency","fix":"Exclude paused time from the latency SLI and track approval time separately"},{"symptom":"A quality alert flaps between pass and fail","cause":"The score comes from a small sample or an uncalibrated LLM judge","fix":"Increase sample size, calibrate against human labels and alert on a rolling pass rate"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t10-s1","title":"A customer-facing streaming assistant","situation":"A chat assistant streams answers to customers all day. Users complain when the first words take too long or when answers are wrong, not when a request takes 20 seconds in total.","constraints":["High traffic","User-perceived latency","Quality matters"],"unknowns":["What does the baseline p95 first-chunk time look like?","How is answer quality sampled and judged?"],"question":"Which SLIs and alerts do you set?","options":[{"name":"SLIs on availability and p95 time to first chunk with burn-rate paging, plus a sampled quality pass rate as a ticket alert","verdict":"best","why":"It measures what users feel, pages on fast budget burn and keeps the noisy quality signal at ticket level.","tradeoff":"You maintain several SLOs and an online evaluation pipeline."},{"name":"A single p95 total-duration threshold","verdict":"poor","why":"Total duration scales with answer length and ignores first-token delay and quality.","tradeoff":"Fine for a non-streaming batch endpoint."},{"name":"Page on every quality score drop","verdict":"good","why":"Quality failures get attention.","tradeoff":"Noisy and may wake people for judge variance."}],"flips":[{"if":"Answers are not streamed","then":"Use inference duration percentiles."},{"if":"The judge is validated against human labels","then":"Promote the quality SLI to a paging signal."}],"takeaway":"Measure what users feel and page on budget burn."},{"id":"w15-t10-s2","title":"A nightly agent pipeline with a deadline","situation":"A batch of 5,000 agent tasks must finish by 06:00. Individual task latency is irrelevant. Failures should be retried automatically.","constraints":["Batch deadline","Few large runs","Automatic retries"],"unknowns":["How many failures can retries absorb?","How early must an on-call know?"],"question":"How do you define health and alerts?","options":[{"name":"SLI: share of tasks completed by the deadline, ticket alert on projected shortfall, page when the projection crosses a hard limit","verdict":"best","why":"It tracks the outcome the business cares about and gives time to react before the deadline.","tradeoff":"Needs a projection of progress, not only counts."},{"name":"Per-task p95 latency alerts","verdict":"poor","why":"Latency of single tasks does not predict whether the batch meets its deadline.","tradeoff":"Useful as a diagnostic after the deadline slips."},{"name":"Alert only when the batch fails","verdict":"good","why":"Simple.","tradeoff":"Learns of a missed deadline too late."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tasks are retried and failures cluster","then":"Add per-tool error SLIs to find the cause."},{"if":"The deadline moves to hourly","then":"Use burn-rate windows."}],"takeaway":"Pick the SLI that matches the promise you made."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"An SLO of 99.5 percent over 30 days is in force. In the last hour the error rate was 7.2 percent. What is the burn rate?","answer":14.4,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"x","hint":"Error budget is 1 minus the SLO.","explain":"The error budget is 0.5 percent. Burn rate is observed error rate over budget: 7.2 / 0.5 = 14.4, the page-level rate for a one-hour window."},{"id":"w15-t10-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Why should a latency SLI for an approval-gated agent exclude time paused for human approval?","options":["Approvals are never traced","Approval time is always under one second","Approval waits reflect human response time, not service quality, and would burn the budget unfairly","SLIs must not include p95 values"],"answer":2,"explain":"Human delays are not service latency. Approvals can be traced, they are often long and p95 is a normal SLI statistic."},{"id":"w15-t10-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the steps of setting an SLO-based alert in order.","items":["Choose an SLI that reflects user experience","Measure a baseline from unsampled metrics","Set the SLO and compute the error budget","Create multi-window burn-rate alerts","Review budget spend and tune"],"explain":"The SLI comes first, the baseline grounds the SLO, the budget defines burn-rate thresholds and review closes the loop."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is burn rate?","back":"Observed error rate divided by the error budget (1 minus the SLO). A burn rate of 1 spends exactly the budget over the window."},{"front":"What starting alert numbers does the SRE Workbook suggest?","back":"Page at 2 percent budget in 1 hour (burn rate 14.4) and 5 percent in 6 hours (burn rate 6). Ticket at 10 percent in 3 days (burn rate 1)."},{"front":"Why alert on metrics and not traces?","back":"Metrics are not sampled, so rates are accurate. Traces explain an alert after it fires."}],"related":["w15-t04","w15-t07","w15-t11","w16-t01","w14-t02"],"refs":["r15","r21","tr1"]},{"id":"w15-t11","order":11,"group":"Operating","title":"Online quality monitoring","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Online quality monitoring scores a sample of live traces with reference-free evaluators, such as LLM judges and safety checks, then tracks the scores for drift. Sampling rate, filters and judge cost are the controls, and privacy rules apply to what judges read.","useIf":"Quality failures are silent, production inputs differ from your test set, and you need an early signal between releases.","avoidIf":"You score all traffic with an untested judge, or send regulated content to an evaluator you do not control.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Offline evaluations run on dataset examples that have reference outputs: regression, benchmarking and gating. **Online** evaluations run on production runs and threads, which have **no reference outputs**, so they check quality patterns, safety and anomalies with reference-free judges [[tr5]]. Foundry frames this as the post-production stage: operational metrics, continuous evaluation of sampled live traffic, scheduled evaluation on a test dataset to detect drift, scheduled red teaming and Azure Monitor alerts [[tr2]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"An online scoring loop","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Sample","md":"Pick a share of live traces by random or intelligent sampling.","tag":"cost control"},{"title":"Score","md":"Run reference-free evaluators on the sampled runs.","tag":"judge"},{"title":"Aggregate","md":"Track pass rates and distributions per agent and version.","tag":"metric"},{"title":"Alert","md":"Notify on sustained drops, not single failures.","tag":"ticket"},{"title":"Learn","md":"Add failing traces to the offline golden set.","tag":"feedback"}]},{"t":"p","md":"In Foundry, continuous evaluation is in **preview** (checked 2026-10-13). `ContinuousEvaluationRuleAction(eval_id=..., max_hourly_runs=100)` documents a default limit of 100 runs per hour, and runs are skipped when it is reached, so raise `max_hourly_runs` if results go missing. The project's managed identity needs the Foundry User role. Live traffic can use Random or Intelligent sampling [[tr3]]. **Intelligent sampling** for trace evaluation does exact dedupe, hard filters (broken, truncated or malformed tool-call traces), aggregation and then MinHash farthest-first selection for diversity, runs on local compute with no extra model calls and takes `max_traces` (preview) [[tr4]]."},{"t":"p","md":"LangSmith sets sampling rate, filters and spend limits per attached project or dataset, not per evaluator, and its docs give 10 percent of traces as an example of controlling cost [[tr5]]. Two design points follow. A frozen test set gives a stable drift baseline, while a set built from traces moves with real users. And online scoring reads real prompts, so content capture and personal data rules apply to the judge as well as to the trace."}],"whereToUse":["A production assistant where answer quality can drop after a model, prompt or data change","An agent platform that needs early warning on groundedness or task completion","A safety program that samples live conversations for policy violations","A release process that feeds failing production traces back into the golden set","Drift detection between model versions or retrieval index refreshes"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Score a sample of live traces, with intelligent or filtered sampling for diversity","Use a validated, cheaper judge and batch criteria where the platform allows","Alert on rolling pass rates and add failing traces to the offline set"],"avoid":["Do not score every request with an expensive judge","Do not trust judge scores that were never compared with human labels","Do not send regulated content to an external judge without controls"],"flips":["Content is regulated: run judges inside your boundary on redacted or referenced content","Traffic is tiny: score everything or review manually","A change has shipped: raise the sampling rate for a short window, then lower it"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to monitor quality after launch","head":["Approach","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Continuous evaluation on sampled traffic","Silent failures, live inputs differ from tests","Content cannot leave a boundary","Judge cost, preview status in Foundry [[tr3]]"],["Scheduled evaluation on a frozen set","Stable regression and drift baseline","Real users shift away from the set","Misses unseen failure types"],["User feedback and review of flagged traces","Cheap signal of real dissatisfaction","Silent errors","Sparse and biased"],["Shadow or canary comparison","Comparing a candidate change on real traffic","No second deployment available","Duplicate traffic cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Evaluation results stop appearing for some hours","cause":"Continuous evaluation hit the default limit of 100 runs per hour and skipped the rest","fix":"Raise `max_hourly_runs` or lower the sample rate [[tr3]]"},{"symptom":"The judge cost grows faster than traffic","cause":"Every trace is scored, or many near-duplicate traces are scored","fix":"Sample, dedupe and filter, and use a cheaper judge after checking agreement [[tr4]]"},{"symptom":"Scores look fine while users complain","cause":"The judge is uncalibrated or the sample misses the failing segment","fix":"Compare scores with human labels and stratify the sample by agent, tool and route"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t11-s1","title":"Fifty thousand conversations a day, cost sensitive","situation":"An assistant handles 50,000 conversations a day. Quality is checked only offline. Judge calls are billed per token and the budget is fixed.","constraints":["High volume","Fixed judge budget","Need drift detection"],"unknowns":["How much do near-duplicate conversations dominate?","Is there a validated cheaper judge?"],"question":"How do you monitor quality online?","options":[{"name":"Intelligent sampling with dedupe and diversity, a cheaper validated judge, rolling pass-rate alerts","verdict":"best","why":"It spends the judge budget on diverse, informative traces and tracks drift cheaply.","tradeoff":"Sampling can miss rare failures, so keep a small random slice too."},{"name":"Score every conversation","verdict":"poor","why":"Judge cost scales with traffic and near-duplicates add little signal.","tradeoff":"Right for very low traffic."},{"name":"Random 1 percent sample with no filters","verdict":"good","why":"Simple and cheap.","tradeoff":"Misses rare segments and wastes budget on duplicates and broken traces."}],"flips":[{"if":"A new model or prompt has shipped","then":"Raise the sample rate for a while."},{"if":"Failures cluster in one tool or route","then":"Stratify the sample by that dimension."}],"takeaway":"Spend judge budget on diverse, informative traces."},{"id":"w15-t11-s2","title":"Regulated conversations and an external judge","situation":"An assistant handles confidential case data. The team wants online quality scores. The only judge available today runs in an external service.","constraints":["Regulated data","External judge","Need online signal"],"unknowns":["Can a judge run inside the boundary?","Can inputs be redacted or replaced by references?"],"question":"How do you proceed?","options":[{"name":"Run in-boundary judges on redacted or referenced content, and keep external judges for synthetic data only","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps regulated text inside your controls while still giving a signal.","tradeoff":"More engineering and possibly a weaker judge."},{"name":"Send full conversations to the external judge","verdict":"poor","why":"It moves regulated content outside your boundary.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for synthetic or public data."},{"name":"Skip online scoring and review a small manual sample","verdict":"good","why":"No data movement.","tradeoff":"Low coverage and slow feedback."}],"flips":[{"if":"The vendor meets your data residency and retention rules","then":"An external judge may be acceptable under contract."},{"if":"Only metadata-level checks are needed","then":"Use deterministic checks on non-content signals."}],"takeaway":"The judge reads the same sensitive text the trace would hold."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Why can online evaluation work without ground truth answers?","options":["It uses reference-free judges and safety checks on production traces","It copies answers from the golden set","It waits for users to label every conversation","It only measures latency"],"answer":0,"explain":"Production runs have no reference outputs, so online scoring relies on reference-free judges and safety checks. The golden set is for offline work and user labels are sparse."},{"id":"w15-t11-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are sound ways to control the cost of online scoring? Select all that apply.","options":["Sample live traffic, using filters and dedupe","Use a cheaper judge after validating agreement with a stronger one","Score every request regardless of volume","Cap runs per hour and raise the cap only when results go missing"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Sampling with filters, a validated cheaper judge and hourly caps all control spend. Scoring every request scales cost with traffic."},{"id":"w15-t11-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"Foundry continuous evaluation results stop appearing for several hours at peak traffic. What is the documented default that may explain it?","options":["Traces older than a day are ignored","Evaluators run only on weekends","Sampling is forced to zero above 1,000 requests","A limit of 100 runs per hour, after which runs are skipped"],"answer":3,"explain":"The documented default is 100 runs per hour and runs are skipped once it is reached. Raising `max_hourly_runs` or lowering the sample rate addresses it."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the difference between offline and online evaluation?","back":"Offline runs on datasets with reference outputs. Online runs on production traces with no references, using reference-free judges and safety checks."},{"front":"What is the default cap for Foundry continuous evaluation?","back":"100 runs per hour (`max_hourly_runs`). Runs are skipped when reached. The feature is preview as of 2026-10-13."},{"front":"What does Foundry intelligent sampling do?","back":"Dedupes, filters broken traces, aggregates and selects diverse traces with MinHash farthest-first, on local compute with no extra model calls (preview)."}],"related":["w15-t10","w15-t06","w14-t02","w14-t09","w14-t15"],"refs":["tr2","tr3","tr4","tr5"]},{"id":"w15-t12","order":12,"group":"Operating","title":"Tooling landscape","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Because the GenAI conventions ride on OpenTelemetry, one instrumented app can feed Application Insights, Langfuse, LangSmith, Phoenix or a local dashboard. Pick the backend by ecosystem, the features you need around traces and where your data may live.","useIf":"You must choose, combine or replace a tracing and evaluation backend for LLM applications.","avoidIf":"You pick a tool for its demo and discover later that its transport, data residency or pricing does not fit.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"OTLP is the common door. Backends differ in what they accept and what they add on top of traces: dashboards, evaluation, prompt management and data controls. Check the transport first, then the features, then where data lives [[r17]] [[r18]] [[r19]] [[r14]]. Because the format is shared, you instrument once and choose the backend by configuration, and an OpenTelemetry Collector can fan the same spans out to several backends during a migration [[r18]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Expect attribute mapping, because the GenAI conventions are still evolving: Langfuse maps received OTel traces to its own data model and supports popular GenAI attributes. To filter or aggregate by user or session there, those attributes must be on every span, not only the root, and baggage with a span processor is the documented way, with the warning not to put sensitive values in baggage [[r17]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Onboarding specifics checked 2026-10-13","head":["Backend","How traces arrive","Gotcha"],"rows":[["Langfuse","OTLP at `/api/public/otel` (traces at `/api/public/otel/v1/traces`), HTTP/JSON or HTTP/protobuf, Basic auth, regional endpoints [[r17]]","gRPC is not supported, so a default gRPC exporter fails"],["LangSmith","`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://api.smith.langchain.com/otel` plus an `x-api-key` header, optional `Langsmith-Project` [[r18]]","Give the base URL only: the exporter appends `/v1/traces`, and including it returns 404. Regional and self-hosted hosts differ"],["Arize Phoenix","Accepts OTLP, built on OpenTelemetry with OpenInference instrumentation, with tracing, evaluation, prompt tools and experiments [[r19]]","OpenInference attributes are a second vocabulary beside `gen_ai.*`"],["Application Insights and Foundry","Microsoft OpenTelemetry Distro or Foundry server-side tracing, KQL, Agents (Preview) view, Grafana dashboards [[r12]] [[r14]]","Agents view and some dashboards are preview; sampling defaults apply"]]},{"t":"dials","id":"w15-t12-backend","title":"Choose a telemetry backend","prompt":"Flip the constraints. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"eco","label":"Ecosystem","values":[{"id":"azure","label":"Azure centric"},{"id":"open","label":"Multi-cloud or open"}]},{"id":"need","label":"What you need","values":[{"id":"trace","label":"Traces, metrics and alerts"},{"id":"evals","label":"Plus evals, datasets and prompts"}]},{"id":"ctrl","label":"Data control","values":[{"id":"strict","label":"Stay in your tenant"},{"id":"flex","label":"Hosted SaaS acceptable"}]}],"options":[{"id":"az","name":"Foundry and Application Insights","summary":"Azure native traces, KQL, Agents view."},{"id":"lf","name":"Langfuse","summary":"OTLP over HTTP, own data model."},{"id":"px","name":"Arize Phoenix","summary":"Open source, OpenInference, evals and experiments."},{"id":"ls","name":"LangSmith","summary":"Datasets, experiments and evaluators."}],"scores":{"az":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":2,"why":"Same tenant, identity and Foundry integration."},"open":{"s":-1,"why":"Little benefit outside Azure."}},"need":{"trace":{"s":2,"why":"KQL, Grafana and alerting come with it."},"evals":{"s":0,"why":"Foundry evaluation exists but is mostly preview."}},"ctrl":{"strict":{"s":2,"why":"Data stays in your subscription and region."},"flex":{"s":0,"why":"No extra benefit from hosting choice."}}},"lf":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":0,"why":"Works through OTLP over HTTP, needs protocol setup."},"open":{"s":2,"why":"Vendor-neutral OTLP ingestion."}},"need":{"trace":{"s":1,"why":"Good tracing views."},"evals":{"s":1,"why":"Adds its own data model on top of traces."}},"ctrl":{"strict":{"s":1,"why":"Local deployment is documented, with operations to run."},"flex":{"s":1,"why":"Hosted regions are available."}}},"px":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":0,"why":"Accepts OTLP but uses OpenInference conventions."},"open":{"s":2,"why":"Open source and built on OpenTelemetry."}},"need":{"trace":{"s":1,"why":"Strong tracing views."},"evals":{"s":2,"why":"Evaluation, datasets, experiments and prompt tools in one place."}},"ctrl":{"strict":{"s":1,"why":"Open source, so you can run it yourself."},"flex":{"s":1,"why":"A managed sibling product, Arize AX, exists from the same vendor."}}},"ls":{"eco":{"azure":{"s":-1,"why":"A separate hosted product outside your Azure estate."},"open":{"s":1,"why":"Accepts OTLP from any app."}},"need":{"trace":{"s":1,"why":"Solid tracing."},"evals":{"s":2,"why":"Datasets, experiments, offline and online evaluators."}},"ctrl":{"strict":{"s":-1,"why":"Hosted by default, self-hosting adds operations."},"flex":{"s":1,"why":"Regional hosted endpoints are available."}}}},"takeaway":"Pick by ecosystem and data control first, then by the evaluation and prompt features you actually use."}],"whereToUse":["Selecting the production backend for agents hosted in Foundry or on Azure","Adding an eval and prompt-management layer on top of existing OpenTelemetry traces","Running a local viewer during development","Fan-out from an OpenTelemetry Collector to two backends during a migration"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Keep instrumentation vendor-neutral with OTLP so the backend can change","Use Application Insights and Foundry when governance, KQL and Azure identity matter most","Add an LLM-focused backend when you need evaluation workflows tied to traces"],"avoid":["Do not choose by demo: check transport (gRPC versus HTTP), residency and retention","Do not send content to a hosted backend before reviewing its data handling","Do not run two backends without deciding which is the source of truth"],"flips":["Data may not leave your tenant: favor Azure native tooling or self-hosted open source","Your team lives in notebooks and experiments: an eval-centric tool outweighs KQL convenience","A short evaluation period: fan out to both backends through a collector, then retire one"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Build versus buy","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Azure native (Application Insights, Foundry)","Azure estate, governance and KQL","Multi-cloud estate or eval-first workflows","Preview parts of the Agents view and some tracing paths [[r14]]"],["Managed SaaS (for example LangSmith or hosted Langfuse)","Fast start, built-in evals and prompts","Strict residency or content rules","Vendor data handling and pricing"],["Self-hosted open source (Phoenix, Langfuse)","Control over data and cost","A small team with no platform capacity","You run, scale and secure it"],["Build on a collector plus Grafana","You already run observability platforms","You need LLM-specific eval workflows","Dashboards and schemas you build and maintain"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Langfuse shows nothing from a default Agent Framework Python app","cause":"The framework's default OTLP exporter uses gRPC and Langfuse accepts only OTLP over HTTP","fix":"Set the protocol to http and use the `/api/public/otel` endpoint with the required headers [[r17]] [[r10]]"},{"symptom":"LangSmith returns 404 for every export","cause":"The endpoint included `/v1/traces` on a base-URL setting, so the exporter appended it again","fix":"Set the base `https://api.smith.langchain.com/otel` and let the exporter add the path [[r18]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w15-t12-s1","title":"An Azure-only company with agents in Foundry","situation":"A company runs everything in Azure. Agents are hosted in Foundry plus a few services on Container Apps. Security wants data to stay in the tenant. The team has KQL experience.","constraints":["Azure-only estate","Data stays in tenant","Governance is a priority"],"unknowns":["Are workflow agents in use (preview paths)?","Is an eval workflow needed on top of traces?"],"question":"Which backend do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Foundry tracing with Application Insights, plus OTel export from the container services","verdict":"best","why":"It keeps data in the tenant, covers hosted agents with no code and gives KQL and Grafana.","tradeoff":"Preview parts remain, and eval workflows are less polished than eval-first tools."},{"name":"A hosted SaaS eval platform for all traces","verdict":"poor","why":"It moves trace data out of the tenant, which security ruled out.","tradeoff":"Right when residency rules allow and evals are the main need."},{"name":"Self-hosted open-source tool in the tenant","verdict":"good","why":"Keeps data in your tenant and adds eval features.","tradeoff":"You operate another platform."}],"flips":[{"if":"Eval and prompt workflows become the top need","then":"Add an open-source tool in the tenant and fan out through a collector."},{"if":"The estate becomes multi-cloud","then":"Keep OTLP instrumentation and revisit the backend."}],"takeaway":"Let ecosystem and data control decide before features."},{"id":"w15-t12-s2","title":"A research team iterating on prompts across clouds","situation":"A small team runs agents across two clouds and iterates on prompts weekly. They want datasets, experiments and trace views, and have no platform engineers.","constraints":["Multi-cloud","Eval and prompt iteration is the need","No platform capacity"],"unknowns":["Is data sensitive?","What volume will traces reach?"],"question":"How do you set up tooling?","options":[{"name":"Vendor-neutral OTLP instrumentation sending to a managed eval-centric backend","verdict":"best","why":"Little to operate, quick datasets and experiments and the backend can change later.","tradeoff":"Data handling and pricing depend on the vendor."},{"name":"Build dashboards on a collector and Grafana","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs platform work the team does not have and gives no eval workflow.","tradeoff":"Right for teams that already run observability platforms."},{"name":"Self-host an open-source backend","verdict":"good","why":"Control and no per-trace fee.","tradeoff":"Operations load for a team without platform engineers."}],"flips":[{"if":"Data becomes sensitive","then":"Self-host or restrict content capture."},{"if":"Volume grows beyond vendor pricing","then":"Move to a self-hosted option."}],"takeaway":"Small teams buy what they cannot afford to operate."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w15-t12-q1","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A Python agent using the default OTLP exporter sends nothing to Langfuse. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The default exporter uses gRPC and Langfuse accepts only OTLP over HTTP","Langfuse requires prompts to be stored first","Langfuse does not support OpenTelemetry","The agent has too many tools"],"answer":0,"explain":"Langfuse supports OTLP over HTTP with JSON or protobuf and does not support gRPC. It does accept OpenTelemetry, and prompts and tool counts are unrelated."},{"id":"w15-t12-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each backend to a documented onboarding detail.","pairs":[{"l":"Langfuse","r":"OTLP over HTTP at `/api/public/otel`, no gRPC"},{"l":"LangSmith","r":"Base endpoint `https://api.smith.langchain.com/otel` with an `x-api-key` header"},{"l":"Arize Phoenix","r":"Built on OpenTelemetry with OpenInference instrumentation"},{"l":"Aspire Dashboard","r":"Local viewer with OTLP on 4317 and UI on 18888"}],"explain":"These are the documented onboarding details checked on 2026-10-13. Mixing them up leads to the 404 and gRPC failures listed in the pitfalls."},{"id":"w15-t12-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Security requires that no trace data leave your Azure tenant, but the team wants evaluation workflows. Which choice fits best?","options":["A hosted SaaS tool for all traces","Application Insights plus a self-hosted open-source eval tool inside the tenant","Disable tracing","Send traces to a public endpoint with content capture on"],"answer":1,"explain":"Data control rules out hosted SaaS, and the eval need rules out nothing. A tenant-hosted combination meets both. Disabling tracing loses the evidence and a public endpoint with content is the opposite of the requirement."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which transport does Langfuse accept for OTLP?","back":"HTTP with JSON or protobuf at `/api/public/otel`. gRPC is not supported."},{"front":"How should you set the LangSmith OTLP endpoint?","back":"`https://api.smith.langchain.com/otel` as a base URL with an `x-api-key` header. Do not add `/v1/traces` because the exporter appends it."},{"front":"What is Arize Phoenix built on?","back":"OpenTelemetry, with OpenInference instrumentation. It offers tracing, evaluation, prompt tools and experiments."},{"front":"How do you stay vendor neutral?","back":"Instrument with OpenTelemetry and OTLP, keep content policy outside the vendor choice and use a collector to fan out when migrating."}],"related":["w15-t02","w15-t08","w15-t09","w15-t11","w14-t08","w12-t14"],"refs":["r10","r12","r14","r17","r18","r19"]}]});
